===== checks/readers/13-profit-is-not-cash/agy-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It’s practical, sobering, and thankfully free of the toxic hustle optimism I usually see in business books. In Ukraine, I understood corporate finances, but doing the math with my own limited refugee savings here in the Netherlands feels entirely different. This chapter made me realize that even if I do everything right and find clients, simply waiting to get paid could destroy my family's stability.

### 2. What landed
The warning about growth hit home hard: "selling more at a profit is exactly what empties the account." When you have almost no safety net, the idea that success can bankrupt you is terrifying but true. Also, the instruction to "Separate the money that is not yours" (like VAT) is the exact financial discipline I need; I cannot afford to accidentally spend the Dutch government's money. Finally, the Dutch legal section was incredibly valuable. As a newcomer, I feel like I have zero leverage, so learning that art. 6:119a lid 6 caps payment terms at 30 days for large companies gives me a baseline of protection I didn't know existed.

### 3. What didn't
The entire Instacart example felt completely detached from my reality. Reading that "the 2022 profit includes a '$358 million tax benefit'" lost me. I am worried about bridging a few thousand euros to buy groceries and pay rent, not moving hundreds of millions on paper. Even though the author admits "The case is enormous and the reader is not," using an American tech giant felt like the kind of Silicon Valley fantasy I actively want to avoid. I also felt slightly talked down to when the author warned that quoting the law at a customer "is a way to be paid once and never engaged again." I built a career in Eastern Europe; I already know how power dynamics and business diplomacy work.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, the "two bridges" section just felt like standard accounting theory I remembered from my old job. On the second read, the arithmetic of Day 0, Day 30, and Day 90 really sank in. I realized that if I take on even a small handful of concurrent jobs, a huge chunk of my emergency savings will literally be trapped in other people's bank accounts for months. It shifted from being a theoretical lesson to a very real, immediate threat to my family's survival. 

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because "The Business That Does Not Invoice" sounds like it might offer exactly the kind of upfront-payment business model I need to minimize my financial risk.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Drop the Instacart SEC filing and replace it with a real example of a small local business dealing with a €10,000 cash gap; seeing how someone with my exact constraints actually navigates this would be infinitely more useful than looking at billion-dollar corporate maneuvers.

===== checks/readers/13-profit-is-not-cash/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

I felt like I'd walked into someone else's meeting. Instacart, $373 million, Dutch law in Dutch — none of that is my café. But the bit about VAT sitting in the account "flattering the balance until the day it leaves" made me go cold, because that's exactly what I've seen owners do, twice, and I've watched them get the letter. So I'm irritated and a bit frightened at the same time.

**2. What landed**

"On paper March was a good month. In the bank, March was expensive and May was the relief." That's twenty-five years of my working life in two sentences. I've stood in kitchens where the owner said we'd had a record month and then asked if the wages could go in Friday instead of Thursday.

"Including the money that was never yours — VAT collected, payroll taxes withheld — which sits in the account flattering the balance." I'm going to write that on the wall of the office. The Monday task "Take today's bank balance and subtract the VAT and payroll taxes you are holding for someone else. That smaller figure is what you actually have" — I can do that on the back of an envelope. No spreadsheet.

"Hold a buffer sized to the gap you have measured, not to a feeling." Fair. I've been sizing mine to a feeling.

And I liked "Quoting the Burgerlijk Wetboek at a large customer's accounts department is a way to be paid once and never engaged again." That's someone who's actually dealt with people, not just read about it.

**3. What didn't**

The Instacart section. "net cash provided by operating activities was $99 million, which consisted of net loss of $74 million, adjusted for certain non-cash items of $38 million" — I read that three times and my eyes just slid off. Then the table of six lines. I don't know what a deferred tax benefit or a valuation allowance is, and the author doesn't stop to tell me. I felt like the daft one at the table.

The whole chapter is written for someone who invoices. "For the last ten jobs: the first material cash outflow, every later direct outflow, the invoice date and each receipt." I don't have jobs. People hand me a card, the money's in the bank in two days. The author actually admits this — "The timing half has no reader-scale case" — and then points me to the next chapter, "The Business That Does Not Invoice". So why is that one half a chapter and this one the full one?

The Dutch box. Half a page of Dutch statute I can't read and don't trade under. I skipped it.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

Second time I noticed the line I'd skimmed past: "When customers pay before you incur the costs — deposits, prepaid subscriptions, retail takings, a supplier who waits — growth can supply cash instead." Retail takings. That's me. So the danger runs the other way for a café: takings come in and make me feel richer than I am while the brewery, the rent and the VAT are all still owed. The chapter says that but only once, in passing, and I nearly missed it. The Instacart bit also made a little more sense second time — the point is just "the two numbers agreeing is luck" — but it took a lot of reading to get one sentence out.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes, but only because the next one's title says it's about people like me, and I want to see if the author means it.

**6. One thing to change**

Cut the Instacart quotations down to the one sentence they prove, and put the retail-takings version of the danger — cash arriving before costs, feeling flush while owing the VAT — up front in Move 1 beside the invoicing version, so a shop or a café can see themselves in the first page rather than hunting for one clause in Move 3.

===== checks/readers/13-profit-is-not-cash/claude-lagos.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

I felt seen and then dropped. The first two pages describe my life exactly — I pay the tailor and the fabric man before any customer pays me — and then the chapter walks off into Instacart and Dutch law and never comes back to a business like mine. By the end I had learned something real, but I had to dig it out from under a lot of pages meant for somebody else.

**2. What landed**

"Growth magnifies the cash cycle, in whichever direction that cycle already runs." That is the whole story of my December. I sold more than ever, made "profit" on every piece, and had no money in January because every naira was sitting in fabric, in the tailor's hands, and in customers who said "I'll pay Friday."

"When customers pay before you incur the costs — deposits… growth can supply cash instead." This is the one I will actually use. I already take deposits on custom orders because I have to; I never understood that this was a *financial strategy* and not just me being desperate. Now I'll take full payment up front on more things, and stop feeling apologetic about it.

"Separate the money that is not yours." I don't collect VAT the way they mean, but I hold money for customers whose orders are not finished yet, and I spend it like it's mine. That sentence stung.

"Can I afford this customer is a different question from is this customer profitable." Yes. The big-order customer who wants credit is the one who nearly finished me.

The "What would prove this chapter wrong" section — an author admitting the advice has a cost, that insisting on deposits can lose you customers. I trust the author more for that.

**3. What didn't**

"The clearest published demonstration is in a document you will never file. Instacart's 2023 registration statement…" — three paragraphs of $242 million and "deferred tax benefit of $373 million." I read it twice and I still could not tell you what a valuation allowance is. Why should I? The author even admits "the case is enormous and the reader is not." So why is it a third of the chapter?

"On Dutch ground" — the whole box. Burgerlijk Wetboek, art. 6:119a lid 5, quoted in Dutch. This is nearly a full page of a chapter I was told was for me. In Lagos the person who owes me money is a girl I know from Instagram. There is no statute. There is no 60-day cap. There is "she blocked me." The author says advice for Americans assumes things that don't exist — well, this assumes things that don't exist even harder.

"Invoice as soon as the contract and the work allow." I don't invoice. Nobody I sell to has seen an invoice. Chapter 13½ is called "The Business That Does Not Invoice" — so the author knows, and still wrote this whole chapter as if I do.

"Hold a buffer sized to the gap you have measured, not to a feeling." In an economy where the naira loses value monthly, holding cash *is* a cost the chapter doesn't mention. My "buffer" is fabric stock, because fabric holds value and cash doesn't.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First read, the Instacart section bored me and I skimmed it. Second read I caught the one line that mattered: "a supplier whose receivable is Instacart's 'timing', and who is very likely your size… you are just more often the vendor being timed." That is the point of the whole case and it is buried at the end of it. Second read also showed me the Monday list is genuinely good — the first one (lay ten jobs on one timeline, find the peak overlap) I can actually do with my WhatsApp order history — and the "two bridges" idea is simpler than it looks. But I still had to be told twice.

**5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?**

Yes — because the next one is literally titled for me, and I want to see if the author actually meant it.

**6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change**

Cut the Instacart numbers to one paragraph ending with "you are just more often the vendor being timed," and spend the saved space on one worked example of a business that takes deposits, holds stock, and gets paid by transfer — no invoices, no statute — so the reader who isn't Dutch and isn't Instacart has something to measure her own gap against.

===== checks/readers/13-profit-is-not-cash/codex-dev.md =====
## 1. Gut reaction after first read

This is a useful explanation of a problem I intellectually understood but probably underestimated when imagining leaving tech for a services business. The concurrent-jobs example made the risk concrete. Still, the chapter felt overlong: it proved the same point several times, then veered into Dutch commercial law that does not apply to me.

## 2. What landed — specific passages

“A profitable business can run out of money on a Tuesday” is a strong opener because it turns an accounting distinction into an operational failure with a date attached. I have little patience for abstract business-book warnings; payroll failing on Tuesday is specific.

The best section is the €6,000 cost, €10,000 invoice, ninety-day collection example. Scaling it from one job to six concurrent jobs, then twelve, clearly demonstrates why “selling more at a profit is exactly what empties the account.” That corrected a bias I recognize from software: assuming favorable unit economics mean scale is automatically healthy.

I also liked the two bridges: “profit → operating cash” and “operating cash → the change in the bank balance.” That gives me a reusable debugging model. It resembles tracing a metric through two systems instead of treating the final discrepancy as magic.

“Separate the money that is not yours” also landed. Subtracting VAT and payroll taxes from the visible balance is an actionable rule, not motivational filler.

## 3. What didn't

The Instacart section is credible but overworked. After the $74 million loss/$99 million operating-cash contrast, I understood the point. The H1 2023 coincidence, full-year reconciliation, deferred-tax detail, repeated quotations, and summary figures felt like evidentiary overkill. Worse, Instacart’s scale and leverage are remote from the side business I might actually start. The author admits this later—“The case is enormous and the reader is not”—but that disclaimer also exposes the missing case I wanted: a small consultancy, agency, or contractor nearly missing payroll.

The Dutch-law box lost me almost immediately at “Dutch law caps payment terms between businesses.” As a US reader, several paragraphs of untranslated statutory language are dead weight. It made me feel like I had wandered into a country-specific edition without being told.

I also doubted “everything on the second bridge is a decision you made.” Loan repayments and equipment purchases may follow earlier commitments or operational necessity; calling them simply decisions understates constraint.

Finally, “What would prove this chapter wrong” does not really offer falsification. A customer lost over deposits would show a tradeoff, not disprove the accounting or cash-cycle argument.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I mainly retained “growth can consume cash” and the €36,000-to-€72,000 example. On the second, I noticed the chapter is more intellectually honest than its directive tone initially suggested: it explicitly admits the lack of a reader-scale case and that “cash discipline is presented here as free, and it is not.” Those admissions increased my trust, but they also made the missing analysis of customer loss, financing cost, and foregone investment more conspicuous.

## 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the customer-value question is exactly the missing half, though I would skim any jurisdiction-specific legal box.

## 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change

Replace half the Instacart analysis and most of the Dutch statute quotation with one documented small-business cash timeline showing invoices, payroll, negotiated terms, peak funding need, the intervention chosen, and its actual cost.

===== checks/readers/13-profit-is-not-cash/grok-lagos.md =====

===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/agy-refugee.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**
I felt seen, but also a bit like a laboratory specimen. As someone trying to start a modest baking business here in the Netherlands without the safety net of my old corporate job in Kyiv, it was a relief to read a business book that admits most small trades don't have slick software or guaranteed salaries. However, the author’s academic tone made my daily survival feel like a theoretical puzzle.

**2. What landed**
The advice to measure the floor in units instead of a fixed money threshold landed perfectly: "how many of the thing you sell, at this week's cost of making it, cover this week's rent, this week's supplier and the minimum you take home." With supermarket prices constantly changing and my savings shrinking, static financial projections (like I used in my old marketing job) don't work for my home-kitchen budget. 

I also deeply appreciated the bluntness about risk: "there may be no salary to return to... the reader with none is running every experiment in this book at a higher stake than any of its cases did." It validated my anxiety. Western business books usually assume you can just "fail fast" and go back to a comfortable office job. As a refugee on temporary protection, I cannot.

**3. What didn't**
I felt alienated by the constant apologies for not having data, which framed my reality as an academic void. The author writes, "This is the one chapter in the book whose case is an absence, and the absence is the finding." My livelihood isn't an "absence"—it's just happening in Ukrainian Telegram groups instead of public stock filings. 

Also, the advice on finding customers—"The school gate at half past eight clears all four. So does the church group, the estate's chat"—felt out of touch for a foreigner. My Dutch is still clumsy, I don't go to the local church, and I am certainly not integrated enough to be in the Dutch neighborhood WhatsApp group. Building trust when you are an outsider is a much steeper climb than just standing at a school gate.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**
The first time, I was annoyed by the author's obsession with finding a "case" to prove their points, feeling it was too detached and clinical. The second time, I realized this is actually a sign of respect. The author refuses to feed me fake, American-style hustle-culture success stories, which I despise. By admitting what they don't know, they strip away the motivational fluff and leave me with a clean, strict framework—the Monday checklist—which I can actually use.

**5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?**
Yes, because the Monday checklist at the end convinced me the author respects my time and won't insult my intelligence with empty optimism.

**6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change**
Stop apologizing for the lack of a formal "case study" and just go interview three actual immigrants running cash businesses at a local market; our notebooks and bank apps are real evidence, even if they aren't published online for VC founders to read.

===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

Well, this is the first chapter of the thing that's actually about me, and it opens by telling me the rest of the book wasn't. I appreciated the honesty and I was a bit narked at the same time — thirteen chapters in and now you tell me. But it reads like someone who's stood behind a counter, not someone who's read about one, and that's rare.

**2. What landed**

"Your supplier is paid before your customer is" — that's the whole job in nine words. I've watched three restaurants go under on exactly that and none of the owners could have said it that plainly.

"Stock bought on a quiet page is the way this trade dies while its shelves look full." I've seen the walk-in stacked with a fortnight of dairy on a wet Tuesday. That line I'd put on the wall.

The floor in units. I don't want to know I need £2,100 a week; I want to know I need to sell 340 coffees and 90 lunches. That I can count on a Saturday and feel it in my stomach by two o'clock. And "keep the old number beside the new one" — that's the bit I wouldn't have thought of.

"A screenshot of a transfer is not money until it clears" and "'I'll take it' in a chat... worth zero." Twenty-five years of people saying they'll book the function room.

The three you turn away as menu, price and hours. "The seven o'clock full English you do not serve" — I've already had that argument with my sister. Nice to have it framed as a decision that buys something rather than me being awkward.

**3. What didn't**

"Move 1 The situation / Move 2 What actually happened / Move 3 The reasoning" — the scaffolding is clanky and I'm being asked to care about the book's method more than my café. Move 2 is a page and a half explaining why there's nothing in Move 2. "This is the one chapter in the book whose case is an absence, and the absence is the finding" — that's the sort of sentence that gets said on a stage with a headset mic.

"Read against the same week last year" — I don't have a last year. Nobody opening does. The chapter's for people starting, and then it hands me an instrument that needs a year of history.

Webvan, Everpix, Cydoc, Turing, Galperin's retreat — I'm told I'm the reader now and then half the paragraph is names I'd have to go back to look up. "The version of Galperin's retreat available to someone who never had a desk" — who?

"The two-cent arithmetic is airtime" — lost me entirely.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First time, the "no salary to return to" bit felt like it was talking about someone else. Second time I clocked it's me — redundancy money and a loan, nothing to go back to — and "run them smaller and write the stopping rule first" landed harder. I also noticed "the minimum you take home" is quietly inside the floor number, which I'd have left out and then wondered why I was skint. And on the second read the Monday list is the actual chapter; the rest is a preface to it. I'd start there next time.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes, but mostly because the Monday list is short enough to actually do and I want to see if chapter fourteen is as straight.

**6. One thing I'd change**

Cut Move 2 to a paragraph and put the Monday list first. Tell me "there's no case, it's you, here's the five things" and then explain why afterwards for whoever cares. And give the "same week last year" line an alternative for someone in week one — because that's who this chapter says it's for.

===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

I respected the author’s refusal to manufacture a case, but this felt like a long caveat inserted to defend the book’s methodology. The practical checklist was clear, yet I kept wondering why prescriptions like “six weeks” deserved confidence when the chapter explicitly says it has no evidence.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“The businesses that publish are the ones with the instruments already” is the strongest observation here. In tech, we routinely derive startup advice from unusually instrumented companies, then mistake data availability for representativeness. Naming that selection effect builds credibility.

“A screenshot of a transfer is not money until it clears; ‘I’ll take it’ in a chat…[is] worth zero” also worked. That is concrete, falsifiable, and maps cleanly to my world: verbal enthusiasm, survey responses, and even procurement interest are not revenue.

I also liked “things sold, never things made or bought in.” It captures a failure mode I recognize from software side projects: counting features shipped or inventory created instead of customer behavior.

Finally, “a blank cell is a thing you did not write down, not a zero” is excellent operational advice. It is precise and prevents bad data from acquiring false authority.

### 3. What didn’t

I am not the primary reader for cafés, dressmakers, or cash-at-the-door trades, so much of the middle became repetitive: each prior chapter’s instrument gets translated into stalls, faces, units, and notes files. I understood the pattern after three examples.

More importantly, the author sometimes turns acknowledged speculation into suspiciously exact instructions. “Six weeks before you read a direction” and “two attempts across a fortnight” appear unsupported. The chapter says “no figure…is a claim about the world,” but these figures still function as advice about the world.

“Yours is the kind that could not, or did not” also rubbed me wrong. Many small operators can measure but rationally choose not to collect detailed customer data because the administrative cost exceeds its value. Framing them as businesses that simply lack instruments feels mildly condescending.

“Same week last year” is unusable for a new business, while “faces seen before” is a weak demand proxy in tourist, event-driven, or infrequent-purchase businesses.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the second read, I saw that the repetition is partly deliberate: this is an audit of which earlier tools survive outside instrumented companies, not a standalone case chapter. The caveats held up better than I initially thought, especially the admission that salary assumptions raise the stakes for poorer founders. But the unsupported timing rules bothered me more, because the author’s evidence standard makes those arbitrary specifics especially conspicuous.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the methodological honesty earned another chapter, even though this interlude needed tightening.

### 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Rewrite the “Monday” section to label every instruction either logically derived or unvalidated heuristic, and remove exact durations unless the text explains why those durations were chosen.

===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/grok-lagos.md =====
1. Gut reaction after first read
I sat up at the dressmaker on a phone, because that is my shop, not a side note. Then they spent a long stretch explaining why people like me leave no evidence, which felt like being let in so they could say the book was never really about me. The Monday list is the first homework I could actually do tonight on WhatsApp. I also got tired of company names I have never heard of.

2. What landed
“Your supplier is paid before your customer is, your prices move with the supplier’s” — that is my week. “A screenshot of a transfer is not money until it clears; ‘I’ll take it’ in a chat is… worth zero” is the sentence I would screenshot for every girl who says “hold it for me.” “Cash that sits is not standing still” and rebuild the floor “the day the supplier’s price moves” fit Lagos better than any dollar dashboard I have been sold. “Stock bought on a quiet page is the way this trade dies while its shelves look full” is how I burned money on a restock that looked like progress. “Take paid work of any kind and keep the stall on Saturdays” is the only stopping rule I have ever been offered that does not pretend I have a salary waiting. “The boosted post, which stops the day the money does” and airtime versus messaging someone who already paid — that is Instagram, not Silicon Valley.

3. What didn't
Opening with “None of the businesses in this book is one of them” and calling this an interlude, Chapter 13½, made me feel like leftover. Webvan, Instacart, Everpix, ConvertKit, “Galperin’s retreat,” Trello — I do not know these people and I do not care. “The seven o’clock full English you do not serve” is a British café talking over a Lekki DM. “Faces seen before, against the same week last year” is useless here: last year’s naira is a different country, and I may not even have a year. “A number that is rebuilt weekly is still a number, which is more than most owners of this kind of business can state” talked down to me. “No figure in this chapter is a claim about the world” then why should I bet my restock on it? Asking me to keep a year of sales and send it in, unpaid, while admitting my trade is “the harder test” the book did not run, felt extractive. I sell from a phone. I do not have a stall lease. I resell as much as I sew. The café and the tailor kept being the real example; I was the translation.

4. What changed between read one and read two
First read I was grateful someone finally described the chat. Second read I noticed how much of the chapter is apology and method, not instruction, and that “same week last year” and six weeks of waiting collapse under inflation. The honesty about no case impressed me more the second time — they did not invent a fake success story — but it also made the advice feel thinner. The Monday tasks looked more usable once I ignored the café.

5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes — “What a Customer Is Worth” is the question my Instagram already asks me every night — but I will skim anything that needs a filing or a salary.

6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Do not write my trade as a translation of a high-street café. Make the phone-and-transfer shop the example, drop last-year comparisons, and put the unit floor in a tiny worked week (this week’s fabric, data, generator, what I must take home) instead of telling me it “does not scale.”

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/agy-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
I felt a deep sense of relief reading this. Most Western business advice I've seen is full of aggressive, American-style hustle optimism that expects you to project millions in future sales and ignore immediate risks. This chapter was like a cold, sobering bucket of water—it respects reality, hard math, and survival over blind faith. 

### 2. What landed
The insistence on counting only "contribution to date" rather than projected lifetime value landed perfectly. Back in Ukraine, I worked in accounting, and I know firsthand that you cannot pay rent with a customer's projected future loyalty. The warning that "your labour has a price even when no money moves" also struck a nerve. With my time stretched thin between learning Dutch, navigating a new system, and managing family obligations, treating my own unbilled hours as a strict financial cost is exactly how I need to protect myself. 

### 3. What didn't
I felt entirely excluded by the Cydoc case study. The author quotes: "a single EHR integration costs around $4,000 to $6,000." I am looking to start a small, manageable business here in the Netherlands—perhaps local marketing or bookkeeping—because nobody here values my foreign credentials. I am not building a seven-year medical AI startup. When the text started analyzing an 11-year payback period for software, I almost stopped reading. It felt like a Silicon Valley problem for people with massive financial safety nets, completely detached from the reality of someone who needs a business that can feed a family this year.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I was so irritated by the tech-heavy AI example that I missed the underlying mechanics. On the second read, I forced myself to look past the medical software jargon and realized the "Monday" section at the end is just a pure, ruthless spreadsheet exercise. I saw how the exact same logic—deducting setup time, support, and acquisition costs before calling a customer profitable—maps perfectly to a simple service business. 

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because seeing that the next chapter is "Paying Yourself" tells me the author actually understands the most terrifying and important part of starting a business.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Drop the seven-year AI startup case—or at least balance it—by including an example of a boring, traditional service business so ordinary readers with limited savings don't have to work so hard to translate the lesson to their reality.

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

Right — a woman spent seven years building software for doctors and it never had a chance, because the thing cost her seventy quid a month and she could only charge under a hundred. That I understood without anyone explaining it to me. The eleven years line stopped me dead; that's a business that's already dead and nobody's told it. But it's all software, and I kept waiting for someone in a café or a shop to turn up and they never did.

**2. What landed**

"Thirty dollars a month against four thousand up front." That's the whole chapter in nine words and I'd have got it stood at a bar.

"A paying customer and a worthwhile customer are not the same thing." I've had customers like that in every restaurant I ran. The party of twelve who book the back room, run you ragged for three hours, drink tap water. You're rushed off your feet and you've lost money. Nobody's ever put a name to that for me before.

"Positive is not the same as sufficient." Yes. And this bit — "A business could run on it if everything else about the business were free, and nothing about a business is free." That's the fear I wake up with.

"There is no average customer." The bloggers and the businesses paying the same $69 and being two completely different customers — that's my lunch trade versus my Saturday brunch trade, and I'd never have thought to count them separately.

And I liked being told what the chapter can't prove. "Cydoc cannot tell you what acquisition costs." An author admitting the limits of his own example is the opposite of a TED talk, and it bought him a lot of trust.

**3. What didn't**

The opening of Move 1 assumes something about me that isn't true: "Somewhere you have already multiplied a monthly payment by a number of months and written the result down." I haven't. I've never done that in my life and I don't have monthly payments — people come in, buy a flat white, leave. So the chapter's opening move is correcting a mistake I haven't made, and for a paragraph I thought this book wasn't for me.

"Two customers who each eventually contribute €2,000" — euros, out of nowhere, in a chapter of dollars. Small thing, but it made me wonder if anyone had read it through.

The Monday list is where I stalled. "Carry the outcome status from chapter eleven across as well — renewed, lapsed, still here." I don't have renewals. Nobody subscribes to a café. I can do the segments — regulars, passing trade, the office order — but the whole apparatus of acquisition spend per customer and a payback date per customer doesn't survive contact with a business where nobody's name is written down. And "your own acquisition time valued at replacement cost" is jargon dressed as plain speech; say "what you'd pay someone else to do it."

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First time I read the Cydoc numbers as a sad story. Second time I saw it was a *shape*: price ceiling set by the customer, cost per customer set by someone else, big setup cost you can't recover. And that shape does fit me — my price is set by what people will pay for a coffee round here, my costs by the roaster and the energy company, and my fit-out is my four thousand. The chapter never says this. I had to build the bridge myself.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — "Paying Yourself" is the one I'm most frightened of, and this chapter earned enough credit to get me there.

**6. One concrete thing I'd change**

Add one paragraph applying the same three subtractions to a business with no subscriptions and no customer names — a café, a hairdresser, a garage — where the "customer" is a segment and the "setup cost" is the fit-out. Without it, half your readers will decide the arithmetic is for software people.

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/claude-lagos.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read.**
This is the first chapter in a while that felt like it was written about my actual problem — I sell a dress for ₦25,000 and I have genuinely never sat down and asked what's left after the boosted post, the dispatch rider, and the two returns. The Cydoc arithmetic is clean and I followed it without re-reading. But the whole case is a software business in America with $4,000 integrations, and I kept translating in my head the whole time.

**2. What landed.**
"A paying customer and a worthwhile customer are not the same thing" — that sentence is my last four months. I have customers who order constantly and I now suspect I lose money on every one, because they're in Ikorodu and the rider costs more than my margin.

The four subtractions are the most useful list in the book so far, and "Refunds, credits and concessions... they tend to live in nobody's calculation at all" is exactly right. My returns are not a line anywhere. Somebody sends the dress back, I resell it eventually, and I've never counted the two riders it cost me.

"There is no average customer" with the WP Curve bloggers-versus-businesses split — same $69, two different relationships. That's my "just browsing, send price" DMs versus the woman who buys for her whole office. Same price, and I have been treating them as one number.

And "your labour has a price even when no money moves." I spend maybe three hours a day answering DMs from people who never buy. Nobody has ever told me to put a naira figure on that.

**3. What didn't.**
The costs in this chapter don't move. "$70 per doctor" is a stable number you can subtract from a stable price. Mine aren't. My fabric supplier changed prices twice this quarter, fuel moves, and the dollar rate decides what my imported stock costs before I've sold last month's. Contribution to date computed in naira across six months isn't one number — it's six numbers in six different currencies wearing the same name. The chapter never says a word about this, and for anyone reading from a high-inflation economy that's not a footnote, it's the whole calculation.

"The month in which cumulative contribution first exceeded what you spent" also assumes a payback measured in years is even a meaningful frame. Eleven years is a scandal in this chapter. Here, eleven *months* is a long time to have money tied in stock.

The one place I felt talked down to is small: "Somewhere you have already multiplied a monthly payment by a number of months and written the result down." I haven't. I've never done LTV, never heard the term, and the chapter spends its opening telling me my imaginary spreadsheet is wrong.

**4. What changed between read one and read two.**
First time I read the eleven-year payback as the point. Second time the real point was the sentence about positive-not-sufficient — "positive on the monthly line and could not carry the business, and both of those things were true at the same time for years." That's the frightening one, because my margin is positive and I still can't pay myself. I also noticed the boundaries section admits Cydoc can't tell you acquisition cost — which quietly removes the one subtraction I most needed help measuring.

**5. Would I keep reading?**
Yes — "Paying Yourself" is the chapter I've been waiting fourteen chapters for.

**6. One thing I'd change.**
Add one short passage on what to do when your own costs move faster than your customers' payments — restate contribution in the currency or the input that actually moves (units of fabric, dollars of stock), or say plainly that the method needs adjusting. As written, the whole chapter assumes a stable naira, and it never says so.

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/codex-dev.md =====
## 1. Gut reaction after first read

This is sharper than most business-book treatments of LTV. I liked the insistence on actual cash, attributable costs, and payback time, but the chapter repeats its core point enough that I started skimming before “Monday.”

## 2. What landed — specific passages

“Future expansion is unknown, not zero — and it is also not evidence” is the line I’d keep. I’ve seen teams make a weak customer look strategic by stuffing expansion and referrals into a spreadsheet; separating observed contribution from hypotheses is genuinely useful.

“A free channel that consumes two days of founder time per customer is not free” also lands. As an engineer considering a side business, my time is probably the largest cost I’m most tempted to pretend is zero.

The instruction to write “not yet” instead of forecasting a payback date is concrete and behavior-changing. So is “aggregate by segment and by channel, never once for the business.” Those are operational rules I could put into a spreadsheet this weekend.

## 3. What didn’t

The claim that lifetime value “is worse than useless” feels overstated. Badly estimated LTV is dangerous, but contribution-to-date has its own severe age bias: a two-month-old customer and a two-year-old customer cannot be compared cleanly without some treatment of observation time. The chapter acknowledges the limitation late, but the opening sells certainty the method has not earned.

More seriously, the featured arithmetic appears dimensionally incomplete. The chapter compares “$70 per doctor” and under “$30 a month per doctor” with “$4,000 to $6,000” per practice, then says $4,000 “comes back in a little over eleven years.” That works only for a one-doctor practice. If an integrated practice has ten doctors, the setup payback is roughly fourteen months before other costs. Maybe Draelos’s eleven-year estimate included the actual practice size or additional expenses, but the chapter as written says, “Her estimate and the arithmetic agree” without supplying the denominator.

I also got bored during the repeated restatements that positive contribution is not sufficient. The point was already established by the Cydoc example.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, the Cydoc numbers felt devastating and authoritative. On the second, I noticed the per-doctor/per-practice mismatch and became less confident in the centerpiece. Conversely, the “Boundaries” section held up better than I initially gave it credit for; admitting that the method systematically misses delayed value is unusually honest.

## 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the teaser about pricing the owner’s labor addresses exactly the omission I care about.

## 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Rewrite the Cydoc calculation with explicit units and doctor count: show the complete equation behind the eleven-year payback, or stop claiming the displayed arithmetic independently verifies it.

===== checks/readers/15-paying-yourself/agy-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
I felt a mix of relief and anxiety. Relief because the author doesn’t sell a dream; it’s just cold math about survival, which I respect after hearing too much toxic positivity about "being your own boss." But anxiety hit me hard when I saw the Dutch tax rules—it made starting a business here feel like navigating a minefield when I already have so little room for financial error. 

### 2. What landed
*"The mistake is to ask what you are worth — unanswerable, and slightly demoralising. Ask what the business would have to pay to get the work done."*
This hit home. Coming from Ukraine with a solid professional career, getting rejected for local jobs because my credentials supposedly don't translate has been deeply demoralizing. Detaching my personal worth from the market rate of the tasks feels like a practical, grounding way to approach a new business. Also, the advice to set a hard date on subsidizing the business with unpaid labor works perfectly for me. With my family obligations and temporary status, I cannot afford a "financed period" that never ends.

### 3. What didn't
Where I felt excluded was the section on Dutch tax law for incorporated companies: *"ten minste € 58.000 in 2026... There are routes to a lower figure and they run on evidence, not on assertion."*
As someone who would start as a simple *eenmanszaak* just to test the waters, reading about a mandatory €58,000 salary requirement was terrifying. Even though the author clarifies the difference between legal forms, the heavy focus on this corporate rule made me feel like this book is written for well-funded founders, not someone bootstrapping just to feed their kids. I got bogged down in the Dutch legalese and felt dismissed by the instruction to just take it to a *belastingadviseur*—those advisors cost money I simply don't have.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I panicked that the Dutch government would force me to pay myself a massive salary I wouldn't have. On the second read, my old accounting background kicked in, and I absorbed what the author explicitly states: as an *eenmanszaak*, this doesn't apply to me (*"Trading in your own name... there is no salary and no minimum"*). I finally understood that the author is using the strict corporate rule to illustrate the discipline all owners should apply in their private calculations, not as a literal threat to my specific situation.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because the next chapter is called "Enough," and figuring out exactly what is enough for my family's survival without taking on disastrous risk is my only priority.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Move the dense Dutch tax law box out of the main flow and into an appendix; dropping it into the middle of the chapter derails the practical logic of pricing your own labor with legal jargon that will unnecessarily scare off small-scale beginners.

===== checks/readers/15-paying-yourself/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

This one actually spoke to me, which surprised me, because the last thing I want from a business book is a lecture about what I'm worth. The core point — that if I don't put my own wages in the sums, the café can look like it's working when it isn't — landed hard, and I'd never thought of it in those words. But halfway through it stopped being a book for me and turned into a Dutch tax document, and I sat there thinking, I'm in Yorkshire, love.

**2. What landed**

"a badly paid job that also carries the downside of ownership" — that's the sentence. Twenty-five years I've run other people's places and been paid for it. The thing I'm frightened of is doing the same work for less and calling it freedom. He named it in one line.

"The mistake is to ask what you are worth — unanswerable, and slightly demoralising. Ask what the business would have to pay to get the work done." That's the only bit of any business book I've read that made me feel less anxious rather than more. I can price a chef. I can price a cover manager on a Sunday. I know those numbers already.

The three answers are good, and the middle one is right about me: "it looks like the third from the inside and like the first from the outside. Writing the date down is what separates them." I know the feeling of a bad month feeling like the end and looking fine to everyone else.

And "you discover you are doing forty hours of skilled work and twenty hours you could hire for a fraction, and that the second twenty has been crowding out the first" — that's every general manager I've ever known, me included, doing the rota at midnight.

**3. What didn't**

The Dutch box. "Bent u werknemer in een vennootschap of coöperatie waarin u een aanmerkelijk belang hebt?" I don't read Dutch, and there's a page and a half of it, in the language, with article numbers. I skimmed it the first time and skipped it the second. It's the longest section in the chapter and it's the least use to me, and it made me feel like I'd wandered into somebody else's meeting.

"This chapter has no company case… No owner in this book is shown computing their replacement cost and acting on it, and that debt is real." Honest of him, and I respect it, but he's admitted the one thing I wanted. I don't need another rule. I need to watch one woman do it and see the number.

"a management restatement — a copy of last year's profit and loss with one extra line" — I don't have last year's. I haven't opened yet. Nothing in the chapter tells me what to do with a forecast instead of a history, and that's most people buying a first business book.

**4. What changed between one and two**

First time the Dutch section felt like padding. Second time I saw the argument underneath it — one type of owner is forced to price their own labour, the other can pretend it's free, and that's an accident of paperwork rather than sense. That's genuinely interesting. It's just buried under quotations I can't read. I also noticed on the second pass how careful he is that counting the wage isn't drawing it — first time I'd half-read that as "pay yourself properly," which would have been dangerous advice for me in month three.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — and "Enough" is the chapter I actually want, because what the café must cover and what my house needs are two different frights.

**6. One concrete thing to change**

Cut the Dutch quotations to a couple of lines and a link, and use the space for a worked example: one owner, her list of jobs, four real advert rates, the total, the line added, the answer she got. Show me the arithmetic once and I'll do it myself.

===== checks/readers/15-paying-yourself/claude-lagos.md =====
# Reading as: 24, Lagos, clothes on Instagram and WhatsApp

## 1. Gut reaction after first read

The opening sentence hit me — "a badly paid job that also carries the downside of ownership" is exactly what my aunt says about her shop and exactly what I'm scared of. Then it went to Netherlands, eenmanszaak, gebruikelijkloonregeling, € 58.000, and I was reading someone else's mail for four hundred words. I skipped the Dutch box on read one, and I don't feel bad about it. The Monday list at the end pulled me back.

## 2. What landed

"A business reporting €40,000 of profit before anything is charged for your labour has told you nothing yet." Change the currency and that is my whole life. I count what I made this month as: money in minus what I paid the tailor and the bus and the airtime. Nobody pays me. I thought that was the good part.

"the customer economics of an owner-operated business flatter, and the flattery grows with how hard the owner works" — this is the sentence I'll remember. It says my hustle is hiding the truth, not proving anything.

The three answers, especially the second: "It stops being legitimate when the date is never written down, because then it cannot arrive." I've been in a financed period for two years without knowing it had a name. That reframing alone is worth the chapter.

"you discover you are doing forty hours of skilled work and twenty hours you could hire for a fraction, and that the second twenty has been crowding out the first." I package and I go to the park and I post and I reply DMs at 1am. That twenty is my whole week.

## 3. What didn't

Move 2 is the whole middle of the chapter and it is a foreign tax code. "For an owner who works for an incorporated company in which they hold a substantial interest, the state imposes a statutory method..." I am not incorporated, there is no such rule here, and the sole-trader half — my half — gets three paragraphs whose conclusion is "you're on your own." So the chapter spends its longest section telling me about a discipline I can't have.

"Price each one against several real adverts or quotes. Three or four current rates" — advertised rates for what I do barely exist here, and where they do they're a WhatsApp negotiation, not an advert. And "loaded with what an employer pays on top of the advertised gross" assumes a payroll system I don't touch.

And nothing about inflation. If I price my labour at ₦X today and read the number back in twelve months, the number lied. The chapter treats "last year's profit and loss" as a stable thing to restate. Mine isn't.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

Second read I actually read Move 2's *argument* instead of its facts, and saw the point isn't the Dutch rule — it's "one owner is handed the discipline whether they want it or not, and the other has to supply it themselves." I'm the second owner. That's a real sentence about me. I also caught "Counting it is not paying it," which I'd skimmed as filler and is actually the safety catch: knowing the number doesn't mean draining the account. And the honesty of "This chapter has no company case... that debt is real" made me trust the author more, not less.

## 5. Would I keep reading?

Yes — chapter 16 is called "Enough" and that's the question I actually have.

## 6. One thing to change

Give the sole trader as many words as the bv owner. Move 2's short half needs a real substitute for the missing discipline — how to price your own jobs when there are no adverts, and how to hold the number steady when prices move. Right now the owner with the least help gets the least chapter.

===== checks/readers/15-paying-yourself/codex-dev.md =====
## 1. Gut reaction after first read

The core argument is strong and uncomfortably relevant: if my hypothetical side business “profits” only because I donate senior-engineer hours, I have built an underpaid job, not necessarily a viable company. But the chapter buries that useful test inside far more Dutch tax detail than I need.

## 2. What landed — specific passages

“A business that is profitable only because nobody costed the founder is not profitable” got my attention immediately. I have absolutely evaluated side projects using revenue minus cash expenses while quietly valuing nights and weekends at zero.

“Price the jobs, not the person” is the best practical instruction here. Splitting my work into engineering, sales, support, and bookkeeping avoids the useless question of whether I am personally “worth” my tech-company compensation. The further qualification—costing “the hours a competent replacement would need”—also matters. A bookkeeper would do my bookkeeping faster; another engineer might take longer to understand code I wrote.

The three-answer test is credible because it allows a deliberately subsidized period rather than declaring every early business invalid. “Writing the date down is what separates them” is sharp: a deadline turns founder optimism into a falsifiable plan.

“Counting it is not paying it” also worked. Separating imputed labor cost from household cash withdrawals is exactly the distinction I would want before considering leaving a comfortable salary.

## 3. What didn't

I checked out during the extended Netherlands material. By the time I reached “Article 12a of the Wet op de loonbelasting 1964” and several untranslated statutory quotations, the chapter had shifted from a business argument into jurisdiction-specific documentation. As a US reader, I felt outside the intended audience, and the repeated caveats did not repay the attention required.

The chapter also admits its largest weakness: “No owner in this book is shown computing their replacement cost and acting on it.” That is not a minor “debt”; it means the central method has no demonstrated behavioral outcome. I wanted one worked example showing roles, market rates, loaded costs, adjusted profit, and the resulting decision.

I also doubt that advertised replacement rates fully solve estimator bias. Senior-founder work is unusually bundled and context-heavy. The chapter acknowledges that a successor might “automate, combine or drop” tasks, but the Monday exercise still invites false precision before the operating model has been redesigned.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I reduced the thesis to “pay yourself a market salary,” which sounded simplistic for an early-stage company. The second read corrected me: the author explicitly says to count the cost always but decide separately what to draw. I also appreciated more the warning that replacement means replacing the work system, not cloning the founder—although that caveat deserves to appear earlier and drive the calculation.

## 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the distinction between what the business can cover and what my life requires is exactly the next question I would ask.

## 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change

Replace most of the Dutch statutory box with a one-page, end-to-end case study of a technical founder whose apparent profit changes after pricing each role, including one task automated and one eliminated.

===== checks/readers/16-enough/agy-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It feels very pragmatic and grounded, entirely avoiding the American hustle culture that I find so naive and exhausting. But as someone whose baseline of safety was erased in 2022, the idea of cleanly calculating a "household floor" and confidently setting a "review date" feels like a luxury for people with stable passports and predictable futures. Still, the cold arithmetic appeals to my former professional life.

### 2. What landed 
The line "if you do not name it the business will name it for you" is exactly what I fear; my Dutch language classes, family obligations, and local bureaucracy already consume me, and I cannot afford a business that eats whatever time is left. I also deeply appreciated the strict reliance on reality: "budgets are aspirations and statements are evidence." In my old accounting career in Kyiv, this was a given, but it is comforting to see it demanded here. Calculating the gap between what my household needs and what other income (like my partner's part-time job) provides is the exact survival math I need to measure my risk.

### 3. What didn't 
The entire Pinboard case study felt alien and disconnected from reality. I almost stopped reading when I saw revenue numbers like "$117K, $178K... against funding of zero." This is a tech worker doing "artisanal SaaS stuff" who can casually take a "year of travel for a political organising project." If my business stalls, I cannot take a "reverse sabbatical"; I risk missing our Dutch rent and failing my parents. The author admits this is an "easy" case, but it made me feel the advice assumes a comfortable Western safety net I simply do not have. 

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
The first time, the software example made me defensive and ready to dismiss the chapter. The second time, I looked closer at the author's warning: "flat is cheap in some businesses and expensive in others." I realized that if I start a local service business here, Dutch taxes, inventory, and overhead mean a flat revenue line will actually slowly bankrupt me. The author wasn't just praising the tech company; he was illustrating a mechanism of control, and warning me to figure out my own specific mechanics.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because "You Are the Bottleneck" sounds like the exact trap I will fall into by trying to do absolutely everything myself to save precious capital.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Replace or supplement the Pinboard example with a traditional business (like a local consultancy or a shop) where the owner has actual fixed overhead and family dependents, because a zero-cost software project has no real-world stakes compared to signing a commercial lease.

===== checks/readers/16-enough/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

This one's about me, not about some app. The idea that the business will decide how much of my life it takes if I don't decide first — that's the first thing in this book that made me put the mug down. But it's a long chapter for what's basically one instruction, and there's a stretch in the middle where a man in America with a website I've never heard of gets more room than I do.

**2. What landed**

"A business has no opinion about how much money you need." That's true of restaurants I've run for other people — the building never once asked whether I'd had a day off.

"Naming it once. Worse, because it feels finished." I'd never have thought of that as the danger. I'd have thought naming it was the whole job.

The four numbers, laid out plain: floor, what else covers it, the throw-off, the gap. I've got a husband on a wage and a small pension coming. Nobody has ever told me to *subtract that* before working out what the café has to earn — "leaving it out is how a business gets asked to carry a household it was never the only support for." That's the sentence I'd write on the wall.

And Monday 1: "Not a budget, not an estimate you feel good about — what the household actually spent." I know exactly which of those I'd have done.

**3. What didn't**

Pinboard. Four long paragraphs, revenue figures for eleven years, "$117K, $178K, $181K, $175K, $193K, $160K and $234K" — I glazed over. One man, no staff, no stock, no rent, no gas bill, no chef walking out on a Friday. The author knows it and says so in the Boundaries — "near-zero marginal cost… A business with payroll, stock or premises has fixed costs that rise on their own." So why is he the whole example? Where's the café? That admission arrives after I've already been asked to sit through his numbers, and it half-undoes them.

Then "Time is the second ledger" — one short paragraph telling me to count hours, then straight to "That is why the money comes first." My hours are the thing most likely to break me. It gets a stub.

Small one: the money's in euros throughout and I'm in Yorkshire. €45,000, €70,000. Doesn't stop me reading, but it keeps telling me the book isn't for me.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First time I thought the four-numbers list was the chapter. Second time I saw it's the *review date* — "a threshold rewritten in the month it is about to be tested is not a threshold, it is a mood." That line does more work than the arithmetic.

Second read I also noticed "What would prove this chapter wrong" — the point about businesses that stayed small and got overtaken not writing anniversary posts. An author admitting his evidence is only from survivors buys back a lot of the trust the Pinboard section spent. I skimmed straight past it the first time; it should not be at the back.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — "whether you are building a practice or an asset" is the question I've been avoiding since I handed my notice in.

**6. One concrete thing I'd change**

Replace half the Pinboard arithmetic with one worked example that has staff, stock and a lease — someone's floor, their partner's wage subtracted, the throw-off in actual money. Show me the five-line page filled in, not just described, because I don't know what "grossed up" looks like until I see a number under it.

===== checks/readers/16-enough/claude-lagos.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

The core idea hit me hard — nobody has ever told me to write down what my life costs and make the business answer to it. But the whole thing is in euros, mortgages and pensions, and the example is a man in America earning $200K a year from a bookmarking site and calling it "enough." I read that number twice. In my world €45,000 a year is not a floor, it is a dream, and the arithmetic assumes a currency that stands still.

**2. What landed**

"A business has no opinion about how much money you need. It will absorb capital, hours and attention up to whatever limit you set, and if you set none it will find the limit experimentally — usually at the point where something in your life breaks." That is exactly what my business does. I restock, I boost posts, I answer DMs at 1am, and the limit I found was my health.

"Subtract what already covers it… leaving it out is how a business gets asked to carry a household it was never the only support for." I live with family and I do not pay rent yet. I had been measuring the business as if it must carry everything, and feeling like a failure. It is not carrying everything. That reframe alone was worth the chapter.

"A threshold rewritten in the month it is about to be tested is not a threshold, it is a mood." I have done this maybe five times. Fair hit.

And "count hours against a stated ceiling the same way you count euros against a floor" — my real shortage is hours, not naira.

**3. What didn't**

"Count the floor from twelve months of statements. Download the year, total the outgoings." I do not have twelve months of statements that mean anything. Half my sales come in cash or transfers to my personal account, mixed with family money, and — the bigger problem — what the household spent twelve months ago is not what it costs now. Inflation here has been brutal. Last year's outgoings, added up, tell me almost nothing about this year's floor. The chapter never says the word inflation once, and that is a big absence for a chapter about a number that is supposed to hold still for a year.

Same for the annual review. "Once a year, on a date chosen now" is a cadence for a stable economy. My cost of goods can move in three months. An annual review of my floor would leave me eight months behind.

"Pension payments your employer used to make, sick pay you no longer get, holiday you now fund yourself" — I have never had an employer. That whole line reads to someone who resigned from a corporate job in Europe.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First read, I nearly dropped it at the euros and Pinboard. Second read I saw the chapter is honest about its own limits — "flat is cheap in some businesses and expensive in others," "one case, and it is the easy kind," and the admission that quiet failures don't publish anniversary posts. That honesty bought back a lot of trust. I also caught the distinction I'd skimmed: the labour charge and the household floor are two different tests that can each pass while the other fails. That's a real idea, and it works in any currency.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — the bottleneck chapter is my actual problem and I want to see if that one is written for someone without staff.

**6. One concrete thing I'd change**

Say what to do when the currency moves. One paragraph: how to set an enough number, and how often to recount it, when last year's statements are not evidence about this year's costs — a quarterly recount of the floor, or the floor stated in stable-currency or in goods rather than in a fixed figure. Right now the whole mechanism rests on "last year's spending is a fact," and for me that is the one thing it is not.

===== checks/readers/16-enough/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

This is unusually disciplined for a business book: it treats “enough” as an operating constraint, not a lifestyle slogan. I came away with a useful framework, although the chapter takes too long to extract it from one unusually favorable solo-SaaS example.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“A threshold rewritten in the month it is about to be tested is not a threshold, it is a mood.” That hit. I’ve moved goalposts on side projects—first revenue, then growth, then “strategic potential”—because admitting the original test failed felt worse than continuing. Requiring a threshold, consecutive periods, a deadline, and a precommitted consequence is concrete enough to use.

The distinction between a business that covers the household floor and one that covers the true labor charge is also strong. “Your household is fine and the business is quietly being subsidised by you” describes a lot of supposedly profitable developer businesses. A side project earning $80,000 can feel successful while paying badly for 1,500 senior-engineer hours.

“Time is the second ledger” worked for me too. My limiting resource is more likely to be evenings, attention, or tolerance for support work than cash. Keeping hours as a parallel ceiling makes the framework relevant to someone who already has a comfortable salary and isn’t forced to make the business pay the mortgage immediately.

Finally, “whether you are building a practice or an asset” is exactly the next question I’d want answered.

### 3. What didn’t

The Pinboard section is overextended. Once I understood “stable revenue can be the product,” I didn’t need every annual figure and then several paragraphs qualifying what could or couldn’t be inferred. “Two records, one sequence, and no arrow between them” is intellectually careful, but it also exposes how little the example proves.

More importantly, Pinboard is almost maximally unlike the business I might start: one person, low infrastructure cost, loyal users, no disclosed dependants, and revenue already well into six figures. The chapter eventually admits, “One case, and it is the easy kind,” but too late. I spent much of the case waiting for evidence from a consulting practice, developer tool with platform risk, or SaaS with employees.

“Almost every reader of this chapter has some” other income felt presumptuous. Plenty of founders are the sole household earner, and a partner’s salary is not automatically dependable business-risk capacity.

The Monday checklist is useful but repetitive. By then, the four-number model and annual review rule had already been explained several times.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I thought the chapter’s claim was mainly “choose how much money is enough.” On the second, I saw the more valuable claim: enough is a versioned decision with evidence requirements and an expiration date. I also noticed that the author explicitly acknowledges survivorship bias and says what would falsify the advice. That increased my trust, though it didn’t repair the thin evidence base.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the practice-versus-asset question is more consequential to me than Pinboard’s revenue history.

### 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Cut the Pinboard narrative by half and replace that space with one worked example of a salaried senior developer evaluating a side SaaS: household floor, partner income, taxes, labor charge, hour ceiling, required revenue, review date, and miss consequence.

