===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/agy-refugee.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**
I felt seen, but also a bit like a laboratory specimen. As someone trying to start a modest baking business here in the Netherlands without the safety net of my old corporate job in Kyiv, it was a relief to read a business book that admits most small trades don't have slick software or guaranteed salaries. However, the author’s academic tone made my daily survival feel like a theoretical puzzle.

**2. What landed**
The advice to measure the floor in units instead of a fixed money threshold landed perfectly: "how many of the thing you sell, at this week's cost of making it, cover this week's rent, this week's supplier and the minimum you take home." With supermarket prices constantly changing and my savings shrinking, static financial projections (like I used in my old marketing job) don't work for my home-kitchen budget. 

I also deeply appreciated the bluntness about risk: "there may be no salary to return to... the reader with none is running every experiment in this book at a higher stake than any of its cases did." It validated my anxiety. Western business books usually assume you can just "fail fast" and go back to a comfortable office job. As a refugee on temporary protection, I cannot.

**3. What didn't**
I felt alienated by the constant apologies for not having data, which framed my reality as an academic void. The author writes, "This is the one chapter in the book whose case is an absence, and the absence is the finding." My livelihood isn't an "absence"—it's just happening in Ukrainian Telegram groups instead of public stock filings. 

Also, the advice on finding customers—"The school gate at half past eight clears all four. So does the church group, the estate's chat"—felt out of touch for a foreigner. My Dutch is still clumsy, I don't go to the local church, and I am certainly not integrated enough to be in the Dutch neighborhood WhatsApp group. Building trust when you are an outsider is a much steeper climb than just standing at a school gate.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**
The first time, I was annoyed by the author's obsession with finding a "case" to prove their points, feeling it was too detached and clinical. The second time, I realized this is actually a sign of respect. The author refuses to feed me fake, American-style hustle-culture success stories, which I despise. By admitting what they don't know, they strip away the motivational fluff and leave me with a clean, strict framework—the Monday checklist—which I can actually use.

**5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?**
Yes, because the Monday checklist at the end convinced me the author respects my time and won't insult my intelligence with empty optimism.

**6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change**
Stop apologizing for the lack of a formal "case study" and just go interview three actual immigrants running cash businesses at a local market; our notebooks and bank apps are real evidence, even if they aren't published online for VC founders to read.

===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

Well, this is the first chapter of the thing that's actually about me, and it opens by telling me the rest of the book wasn't. I appreciated the honesty and I was a bit narked at the same time — thirteen chapters in and now you tell me. But it reads like someone who's stood behind a counter, not someone who's read about one, and that's rare.

**2. What landed**

"Your supplier is paid before your customer is" — that's the whole job in nine words. I've watched three restaurants go under on exactly that and none of the owners could have said it that plainly.

"Stock bought on a quiet page is the way this trade dies while its shelves look full." I've seen the walk-in stacked with a fortnight of dairy on a wet Tuesday. That line I'd put on the wall.

The floor in units. I don't want to know I need £2,100 a week; I want to know I need to sell 340 coffees and 90 lunches. That I can count on a Saturday and feel it in my stomach by two o'clock. And "keep the old number beside the new one" — that's the bit I wouldn't have thought of.

"A screenshot of a transfer is not money until it clears" and "'I'll take it' in a chat... worth zero." Twenty-five years of people saying they'll book the function room.

The three you turn away as menu, price and hours. "The seven o'clock full English you do not serve" — I've already had that argument with my sister. Nice to have it framed as a decision that buys something rather than me being awkward.

**3. What didn't**

"Move 1 The situation / Move 2 What actually happened / Move 3 The reasoning" — the scaffolding is clanky and I'm being asked to care about the book's method more than my café. Move 2 is a page and a half explaining why there's nothing in Move 2. "This is the one chapter in the book whose case is an absence, and the absence is the finding" — that's the sort of sentence that gets said on a stage with a headset mic.

"Read against the same week last year" — I don't have a last year. Nobody opening does. The chapter's for people starting, and then it hands me an instrument that needs a year of history.

Webvan, Everpix, Cydoc, Turing, Galperin's retreat — I'm told I'm the reader now and then half the paragraph is names I'd have to go back to look up. "The version of Galperin's retreat available to someone who never had a desk" — who?

"The two-cent arithmetic is airtime" — lost me entirely.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First time, the "no salary to return to" bit felt like it was talking about someone else. Second time I clocked it's me — redundancy money and a loan, nothing to go back to — and "run them smaller and write the stopping rule first" landed harder. I also noticed "the minimum you take home" is quietly inside the floor number, which I'd have left out and then wondered why I was skint. And on the second read the Monday list is the actual chapter; the rest is a preface to it. I'd start there next time.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes, but mostly because the Monday list is short enough to actually do and I want to see if chapter fourteen is as straight.

**6. One thing I'd change**

Cut Move 2 to a paragraph and put the Monday list first. Tell me "there's no case, it's you, here's the five things" and then explain why afterwards for whoever cares. And give the "same week last year" line an alternative for someone in week one — because that's who this chapter says it's for.

===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

I respected the author’s refusal to manufacture a case, but this felt like a long caveat inserted to defend the book’s methodology. The practical checklist was clear, yet I kept wondering why prescriptions like “six weeks” deserved confidence when the chapter explicitly says it has no evidence.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“The businesses that publish are the ones with the instruments already” is the strongest observation here. In tech, we routinely derive startup advice from unusually instrumented companies, then mistake data availability for representativeness. Naming that selection effect builds credibility.

“A screenshot of a transfer is not money until it clears; ‘I’ll take it’ in a chat…[is] worth zero” also worked. That is concrete, falsifiable, and maps cleanly to my world: verbal enthusiasm, survey responses, and even procurement interest are not revenue.

I also liked “things sold, never things made or bought in.” It captures a failure mode I recognize from software side projects: counting features shipped or inventory created instead of customer behavior.

Finally, “a blank cell is a thing you did not write down, not a zero” is excellent operational advice. It is precise and prevents bad data from acquiring false authority.

### 3. What didn’t

I am not the primary reader for cafés, dressmakers, or cash-at-the-door trades, so much of the middle became repetitive: each prior chapter’s instrument gets translated into stalls, faces, units, and notes files. I understood the pattern after three examples.

More importantly, the author sometimes turns acknowledged speculation into suspiciously exact instructions. “Six weeks before you read a direction” and “two attempts across a fortnight” appear unsupported. The chapter says “no figure…is a claim about the world,” but these figures still function as advice about the world.

“Yours is the kind that could not, or did not” also rubbed me wrong. Many small operators can measure but rationally choose not to collect detailed customer data because the administrative cost exceeds its value. Framing them as businesses that simply lack instruments feels mildly condescending.

“Same week last year” is unusable for a new business, while “faces seen before” is a weak demand proxy in tourist, event-driven, or infrequent-purchase businesses.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the second read, I saw that the repetition is partly deliberate: this is an audit of which earlier tools survive outside instrumented companies, not a standalone case chapter. The caveats held up better than I initially thought, especially the admission that salary assumptions raise the stakes for poorer founders. But the unsupported timing rules bothered me more, because the author’s evidence standard makes those arbitrary specifics especially conspicuous.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the methodological honesty earned another chapter, even though this interlude needed tightening.

### 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Rewrite the “Monday” section to label every instruction either logically derived or unvalidated heuristic, and remove exact durations unless the text explains why those durations were chosen.

===== checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/grok-lagos.md =====
1. Gut reaction after first read
I sat up at the dressmaker on a phone, because that is my shop, not a side note. Then they spent a long stretch explaining why people like me leave no evidence, which felt like being let in so they could say the book was never really about me. The Monday list is the first homework I could actually do tonight on WhatsApp. I also got tired of company names I have never heard of.

2. What landed
“Your supplier is paid before your customer is, your prices move with the supplier’s” — that is my week. “A screenshot of a transfer is not money until it clears; ‘I’ll take it’ in a chat is… worth zero” is the sentence I would screenshot for every girl who says “hold it for me.” “Cash that sits is not standing still” and rebuild the floor “the day the supplier’s price moves” fit Lagos better than any dollar dashboard I have been sold. “Stock bought on a quiet page is the way this trade dies while its shelves look full” is how I burned money on a restock that looked like progress. “Take paid work of any kind and keep the stall on Saturdays” is the only stopping rule I have ever been offered that does not pretend I have a salary waiting. “The boosted post, which stops the day the money does” and airtime versus messaging someone who already paid — that is Instagram, not Silicon Valley.

3. What didn't
Opening with “None of the businesses in this book is one of them” and calling this an interlude, Chapter 13½, made me feel like leftover. Webvan, Instacart, Everpix, ConvertKit, “Galperin’s retreat,” Trello — I do not know these people and I do not care. “The seven o’clock full English you do not serve” is a British café talking over a Lekki DM. “Faces seen before, against the same week last year” is useless here: last year’s naira is a different country, and I may not even have a year. “A number that is rebuilt weekly is still a number, which is more than most owners of this kind of business can state” talked down to me. “No figure in this chapter is a claim about the world” then why should I bet my restock on it? Asking me to keep a year of sales and send it in, unpaid, while admitting my trade is “the harder test” the book did not run, felt extractive. I sell from a phone. I do not have a stall lease. I resell as much as I sew. The café and the tailor kept being the real example; I was the translation.

4. What changed between read one and read two
First read I was grateful someone finally described the chat. Second read I noticed how much of the chapter is apology and method, not instruction, and that “same week last year” and six weeks of waiting collapse under inflation. The honesty about no case impressed me more the second time — they did not invent a fake success story — but it also made the advice feel thinner. The Monday tasks looked more usable once I ignored the café.

5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes — “What a Customer Is Worth” is the question my Instagram already asks me every night — but I will skim anything that needs a filing or a salary.

6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Do not write my trade as a translation of a high-street café. Make the phone-and-transfer shop the example, drop last-year comparisons, and put the unit floor in a tiny worked week (this week’s fabric, data, generator, what I must take home) instead of telling me it “does not scale.”

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/agy-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
I felt a deep sense of relief reading this. Most Western business advice I've seen is full of aggressive, American-style hustle optimism that expects you to project millions in future sales and ignore immediate risks. This chapter was like a cold, sobering bucket of water—it respects reality, hard math, and survival over blind faith. 

### 2. What landed
The insistence on counting only "contribution to date" rather than projected lifetime value landed perfectly. Back in Ukraine, I worked in accounting, and I know firsthand that you cannot pay rent with a customer's projected future loyalty. The warning that "your labour has a price even when no money moves" also struck a nerve. With my time stretched thin between learning Dutch, navigating a new system, and managing family obligations, treating my own unbilled hours as a strict financial cost is exactly how I need to protect myself. 

### 3. What didn't
I felt entirely excluded by the Cydoc case study. The author quotes: "a single EHR integration costs around $4,000 to $6,000." I am looking to start a small, manageable business here in the Netherlands—perhaps local marketing or bookkeeping—because nobody here values my foreign credentials. I am not building a seven-year medical AI startup. When the text started analyzing an 11-year payback period for software, I almost stopped reading. It felt like a Silicon Valley problem for people with massive financial safety nets, completely detached from the reality of someone who needs a business that can feed a family this year.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I was so irritated by the tech-heavy AI example that I missed the underlying mechanics. On the second read, I forced myself to look past the medical software jargon and realized the "Monday" section at the end is just a pure, ruthless spreadsheet exercise. I saw how the exact same logic—deducting setup time, support, and acquisition costs before calling a customer profitable—maps perfectly to a simple service business. 

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because seeing that the next chapter is "Paying Yourself" tells me the author actually understands the most terrifying and important part of starting a business.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Drop the seven-year AI startup case—or at least balance it—by including an example of a boring, traditional service business so ordinary readers with limited savings don't have to work so hard to translate the lesson to their reality.

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

Right — a woman spent seven years building software for doctors and it never had a chance, because the thing cost her seventy quid a month and she could only charge under a hundred. That I understood without anyone explaining it to me. The eleven years line stopped me dead; that's a business that's already dead and nobody's told it. But it's all software, and I kept waiting for someone in a café or a shop to turn up and they never did.

**2. What landed**

"Thirty dollars a month against four thousand up front." That's the whole chapter in nine words and I'd have got it stood at a bar.

"A paying customer and a worthwhile customer are not the same thing." I've had customers like that in every restaurant I ran. The party of twelve who book the back room, run you ragged for three hours, drink tap water. You're rushed off your feet and you've lost money. Nobody's ever put a name to that for me before.

"Positive is not the same as sufficient." Yes. And this bit — "A business could run on it if everything else about the business were free, and nothing about a business is free." That's the fear I wake up with.

"There is no average customer." The bloggers and the businesses paying the same $69 and being two completely different customers — that's my lunch trade versus my Saturday brunch trade, and I'd never have thought to count them separately.

And I liked being told what the chapter can't prove. "Cydoc cannot tell you what acquisition costs." An author admitting the limits of his own example is the opposite of a TED talk, and it bought him a lot of trust.

**3. What didn't**

The opening of Move 1 assumes something about me that isn't true: "Somewhere you have already multiplied a monthly payment by a number of months and written the result down." I haven't. I've never done that in my life and I don't have monthly payments — people come in, buy a flat white, leave. So the chapter's opening move is correcting a mistake I haven't made, and for a paragraph I thought this book wasn't for me.

"Two customers who each eventually contribute €2,000" — euros, out of nowhere, in a chapter of dollars. Small thing, but it made me wonder if anyone had read it through.

The Monday list is where I stalled. "Carry the outcome status from chapter eleven across as well — renewed, lapsed, still here." I don't have renewals. Nobody subscribes to a café. I can do the segments — regulars, passing trade, the office order — but the whole apparatus of acquisition spend per customer and a payback date per customer doesn't survive contact with a business where nobody's name is written down. And "your own acquisition time valued at replacement cost" is jargon dressed as plain speech; say "what you'd pay someone else to do it."

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First time I read the Cydoc numbers as a sad story. Second time I saw it was a *shape*: price ceiling set by the customer, cost per customer set by someone else, big setup cost you can't recover. And that shape does fit me — my price is set by what people will pay for a coffee round here, my costs by the roaster and the energy company, and my fit-out is my four thousand. The chapter never says this. I had to build the bridge myself.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — "Paying Yourself" is the one I'm most frightened of, and this chapter earned enough credit to get me there.

**6. One concrete thing I'd change**

Add one paragraph applying the same three subtractions to a business with no subscriptions and no customer names — a café, a hairdresser, a garage — where the "customer" is a segment and the "setup cost" is the fit-out. Without it, half your readers will decide the arithmetic is for software people.

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/claude-lagos.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read.**
This is the first chapter in a while that felt like it was written about my actual problem — I sell a dress for ₦25,000 and I have genuinely never sat down and asked what's left after the boosted post, the dispatch rider, and the two returns. The Cydoc arithmetic is clean and I followed it without re-reading. But the whole case is a software business in America with $4,000 integrations, and I kept translating in my head the whole time.

**2. What landed.**
"A paying customer and a worthwhile customer are not the same thing" — that sentence is my last four months. I have customers who order constantly and I now suspect I lose money on every one, because they're in Ikorodu and the rider costs more than my margin.

The four subtractions are the most useful list in the book so far, and "Refunds, credits and concessions... they tend to live in nobody's calculation at all" is exactly right. My returns are not a line anywhere. Somebody sends the dress back, I resell it eventually, and I've never counted the two riders it cost me.

"There is no average customer" with the WP Curve bloggers-versus-businesses split — same $69, two different relationships. That's my "just browsing, send price" DMs versus the woman who buys for her whole office. Same price, and I have been treating them as one number.

And "your labour has a price even when no money moves." I spend maybe three hours a day answering DMs from people who never buy. Nobody has ever told me to put a naira figure on that.

**3. What didn't.**
The costs in this chapter don't move. "$70 per doctor" is a stable number you can subtract from a stable price. Mine aren't. My fabric supplier changed prices twice this quarter, fuel moves, and the dollar rate decides what my imported stock costs before I've sold last month's. Contribution to date computed in naira across six months isn't one number — it's six numbers in six different currencies wearing the same name. The chapter never says a word about this, and for anyone reading from a high-inflation economy that's not a footnote, it's the whole calculation.

"The month in which cumulative contribution first exceeded what you spent" also assumes a payback measured in years is even a meaningful frame. Eleven years is a scandal in this chapter. Here, eleven *months* is a long time to have money tied in stock.

The one place I felt talked down to is small: "Somewhere you have already multiplied a monthly payment by a number of months and written the result down." I haven't. I've never done LTV, never heard the term, and the chapter spends its opening telling me my imaginary spreadsheet is wrong.

**4. What changed between read one and read two.**
First time I read the eleven-year payback as the point. Second time the real point was the sentence about positive-not-sufficient — "positive on the monthly line and could not carry the business, and both of those things were true at the same time for years." That's the frightening one, because my margin is positive and I still can't pay myself. I also noticed the boundaries section admits Cydoc can't tell you acquisition cost — which quietly removes the one subtraction I most needed help measuring.

**5. Would I keep reading?**
Yes — "Paying Yourself" is the chapter I've been waiting fourteen chapters for.

**6. One thing I'd change.**
Add one short passage on what to do when your own costs move faster than your customers' payments — restate contribution in the currency or the input that actually moves (units of fabric, dollars of stock), or say plainly that the method needs adjusting. As written, the whole chapter assumes a stable naira, and it never says so.

===== checks/readers/14-what-a-customer-is-worth/codex-dev.md =====
## 1. Gut reaction after first read

This is sharper than most business-book treatments of LTV. I liked the insistence on actual cash, attributable costs, and payback time, but the chapter repeats its core point enough that I started skimming before “Monday.”

## 2. What landed — specific passages

“Future expansion is unknown, not zero — and it is also not evidence” is the line I’d keep. I’ve seen teams make a weak customer look strategic by stuffing expansion and referrals into a spreadsheet; separating observed contribution from hypotheses is genuinely useful.

“A free channel that consumes two days of founder time per customer is not free” also lands. As an engineer considering a side business, my time is probably the largest cost I’m most tempted to pretend is zero.

The instruction to write “not yet” instead of forecasting a payback date is concrete and behavior-changing. So is “aggregate by segment and by channel, never once for the business.” Those are operational rules I could put into a spreadsheet this weekend.

## 3. What didn’t

The claim that lifetime value “is worse than useless” feels overstated. Badly estimated LTV is dangerous, but contribution-to-date has its own severe age bias: a two-month-old customer and a two-year-old customer cannot be compared cleanly without some treatment of observation time. The chapter acknowledges the limitation late, but the opening sells certainty the method has not earned.

More seriously, the featured arithmetic appears dimensionally incomplete. The chapter compares “$70 per doctor” and under “$30 a month per doctor” with “$4,000 to $6,000” per practice, then says $4,000 “comes back in a little over eleven years.” That works only for a one-doctor practice. If an integrated practice has ten doctors, the setup payback is roughly fourteen months before other costs. Maybe Draelos’s eleven-year estimate included the actual practice size or additional expenses, but the chapter as written says, “Her estimate and the arithmetic agree” without supplying the denominator.

I also got bored during the repeated restatements that positive contribution is not sufficient. The point was already established by the Cydoc example.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, the Cydoc numbers felt devastating and authoritative. On the second, I noticed the per-doctor/per-practice mismatch and became less confident in the centerpiece. Conversely, the “Boundaries” section held up better than I initially gave it credit for; admitting that the method systematically misses delayed value is unusually honest.

## 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the teaser about pricing the owner’s labor addresses exactly the omission I care about.

## 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Rewrite the Cydoc calculation with explicit units and doctor count: show the complete equation behind the eleven-year payback, or stop claiming the displayed arithmetic independently verifies it.

