The first line got me — a job that takes you fifteen minutes takes the new person an hour. That's every kitchen I've ever worked in, and nobody's ever put it that plainly to me. But then it turned into a lot of talk about deploy scripts and SaaS and folders in Google Drive, and I started thinking, this is a lad who writes software, what's he got to do with me and a coffee machine. Still, I didn't stop reading, which surprised me.

**2. What landed**

"It is also, left unexamined, the single most comfortable form of avoidance available to an owner who does not actually want to hand the decision over." That's a proper slap. I've watched managers do exactly that — write the folder, laminate it, and still stand over the poor lass on the till. I've done it myself.

"Handing a task over for real means watching someone else do it worse than you would, live, in front of a customer who might notice." That's the whole thing, isn't it. That's the bit that costs. Nobody says that out loud.

"I'll delegate once it's properly documented is not a plan. It is a stopping condition with no way to fail it." I understood that immediately, no jargon needed.

And the Monday bit: hand it over, leave the room, write down the time and whether it came out right. That I can do on a Tuesday morning with a pen. "Wrong turns are the point" — yes.

The knowledge-versus-intuition split is right too. I can write down how to do the cash-up. I cannot write down how I know a table's about to kick off before they've said anything.

**3. What didn't**

"why the deploy script has that one flag nobody remembers adding" — I don't know what that is and it told me who this book thinks it's for. Same with "self-funded B2B SaaS at close to the reader's scale." I had to guess at that. And "over 10 FTE" — say ten staff.

The whole "What would prove this chapter wrong" section lost me. "note the asymmetry honestly" — that's the TED talk voice creeping back in. I skimmed it both times.

And the honesty gets a bit much. You tell me three separate times that this fella never actually proved his system worked — in Move 2, in the Boundaries, and in the Note at the bottom. After the second time I thought, well why are you telling me this then. One clear line would do.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

Second time I saw the case study isn't really the point — the point is the sort. Procedure on one side, judgment on the other, and don't waste a quarter writing up the second column. First read I thought this was a chapter about writing manuals; it isn't, it's about not kidding yourself. I also noticed the SOP thing that actually matters: the reps write the entries themselves, on the ticket in front of them. That's the one bit I'd genuinely nick — my staff write the notes, not me. It's buried in a long quote and I missed it entirely first time.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — next one's about the first hire and I'm three weeks off making mine.

**6. One concrete thing I'd change**

Bring "the people using it write it, on the case in front of them" up out of the quote and make it a headed point of its own, in your words, with a non-software example — the barista who writes down the fix for the grinder the day she finds it. It's the most useful idea in the chapter and it's currently hiding inside a block quote about knowledge bases.

===== checks/readers/18-writing-it-down/claude-lagos.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

This one is not for me yet, but I could see it coming for me. I have one girl who helps me pack and reply DMs, and every single day she still calls me to ask "aunty, this customer says the gown didn't fit, should I refund?" — so the fifteen-minutes-versus-an-hour thing hit hard. But the whole chapter is one man with ten staff and a software company, and I kept waiting for someone who looks like me.

**2. What landed**

"A support process that would 'take me a couple of minutes to go through and respond' can, for a rep who is not a developer and has no view of the development queue, 'take close to an hour, and require my participation at each one of those decision points.'" That is my life exactly. I thought I was managing badly. It's not that — the answer just lives in my head.

"I'll delegate once it's properly documented is not a plan. It is a stopping condition with no way to fail it." I do this with everything, not just documents. Website "not ready", price list "not final". That sentence caught me.

The rep writing the entry herself — "Reps input a new entry every time they encounter a ticket" — is the only part I can actually do this week. My girl types faster than me on WhatsApp anyway. She can build it, not me.

And the procedure/judgment split is real. Knowing which supplier at Balogun will actually hold my order without full payment is not something I can write down. That's face and years. Good that the book says so instead of pretending.

**3. What didn't**

"Store it where a new person would actually look, not in a document only you have ever opened." Where? He says Google Drive earlier — with data at ₦-whatever per GB and NEPA gone half the day, my staff is not opening Drive. Everything here runs in WhatsApp. Nobody in this chapter has ever considered that the document might live in a pinned chat, and it makes the advice feel written from a desk with fibre.

"leave the room" — I don't have a room. We work from my mother's parlour and I'm sitting right there. The test as written assumes an office.

Also "an assistant, a contractor, a first hire, however you got there" — that "however you got there" is doing a lot of work. My "hire" is my cousin, unpaid-ish, who may leave next month for school. Documenting for her feels different from documenting for an FTE and the chapter never touches that.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First read I thought the chapter was weak because the man never proves the documents worked — "he does not report a rep working from those SOPs." Second read I respected that. The book says it straight instead of selling me a system, and the "what would prove this chapter wrong" part actually names the danger: someone following the written rule "off the edge of it." That's the risk with my cousin and a refund. First read I skimmed it as filler. It's the most useful paragraph.

I also caught that Monday step 4 tells me to record two numbers before I fix the document. First read I only saw "write it down."

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — chapter nineteen is my actual question, whether to hire at all.

**6. One concrete thing I'd change**

Cut the dependence on the office and the Drive. Say what the handoff test looks like when you can't leave the room and the document has to live where your staff already is — a pinned WhatsApp message, a voice note transcribed, a printed sheet. "Leave the room" should become "don't answer for one hour," which is the version I can actually run.

===== checks/readers/18-writing-it-down/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

This is more credible than most delegation advice because it admits the evidence is weak and gives me an actual test to run. The procedure-versus-judgment distinction felt immediately useful, though the chapter repeats it enough that I started skimming near the end.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“The work moved and he did not” is the sharpest line in the chapter. I’ve seen exactly that failure mode: a task disappears from my queue, but every branch in its decision tree still interrupts me.

“A document does not count as written down until a named other person has used it, unsupervised, on a live instance” also works. That turns “we have docs” from an organizational virtue signal into a falsifiable claim. Recording elapsed time and correctness against my baseline is concrete enough that I could try it with an on-call runbook or customer escalation this week.

I also trusted the author more for saying the Gymdesk remedy’s “result is not yet in.” Business books routinely convert one founder’s retrospective into a universal law. Here, the author clearly separates Galperin’s observed problem from his unproven solution.

### 3. What didn't

“The dividing line is whether the task is the same shape twice” is memorable but too binary. In software, many tasks share a recognizable shape while still requiring judgment: incident response, code review, hiring, enterprise support. Good documentation can transfer heuristics, escalation thresholds, examples, and boundaries even when it cannot “install” intuition. The chapter risks convincing an owner to classify too much as judgment and preserve the very dependency it wants to remove.

I got bored during “Boundaries.” The extended inventory of what belongs to chapters seventeen, nineteen, twenty, and twenty-three reads like book architecture leaking into the argument.

“What would prove this chapter wrong” also feels strained. A bad documented handoff would not necessarily prove the chapter wrong, because the author can always say the procedure was incomplete or the task was really judgment. That makes the advice less falsifiable than the section claims.

Finally, “competent stranger” hides a major variable. Competent in the function, the industry, or this particular system? The answer changes what a successful handoff demonstrates.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I mainly saw a practical warning against documentation theater. On the second, I noticed the more valuable mechanism: the recipient edits the procedure before the founder does, converting each failure into shared operating knowledge. I also became less convinced by the procedure/judgment split; it works as a sorting prompt, but not as a clean model of real knowledge transfer.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the promised chapter on the first hire is close enough to decisions I’ve considered personally.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change

Replace most of the “Boundaries” section with one worked handoff showing the original procedure, where the recipient failed, the two recorded numbers, and the exact revision they made.

