===== checks/readers/22-measuring-the-right-three-things/agy-refugee.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**
I felt a mix of professional recognition and deep irritation. The core accounting logic made sense to my background, but using an American software startup making over a million dollars a month as the main example felt completely disconnected from my reality of trying to start a modest, safe business here in the Netherlands. I almost tuned out when the Silicon Valley jargon started flowing.

**2. What landed — specific passages (quote briefly) and why they worked for someone like me**
The psychological advice about not lying to yourself resonated strongly. "A figure that needs you to explain it is one more thing that only you can do" is brilliant; it reminds me that a real business must eventually survive without my constant micromanagement, which is vital since I have family obligations to juggle. I also deeply appreciated the rule to "fix the definition before anyone is judged by the figure, in writing." In my past corporate life in Kyiv, I saw how easily managers manipulate definitions to make bad months look good. For a low-margin business where I absolutely cannot afford to lose my limited savings, forced honesty is survival.

**3. What didn't — where I got bored, doubted the author, felt excluded or talked down to, or felt the advice didn't fit my situation. Quote the spot**
The entire extended example about ConvertKit alienated me. When the author quotes, "Because our beginner customers churn more often than our larger customers, user churn at ConvertKit is always higher than revenue churn," I felt excluded. I am looking to build a local service business where losing a customer means an empty chair or unsold inventory, not "user churn" on a software dashboard. The American software-as-a-service model assumes high scale and abstract metrics, which is the exact opposite of my cautious, physical reality on a temporary protection visa. It felt like the kind of blind hustle optimism I try to avoid.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**
The first time, the tech jargon ("MRR", "quick ratio") distracted me so much I missed the author's actual framework. On the second read, I realized the author explicitly dismisses those complex software metrics for normal readers: "Net revenue against recurring is a distinction a subscription business needs and most of this book's readers do not." I saw that the core triad—movement, loss, and concentration—is actually very practical and maps perfectly to a traditional, boring business, even if the case study doesn't. 

**5. Would I keep reading the next chapter? One sentence.**
Yes, because Chapter 23 is titled "Slack," and with my current life situation, I desperately need to know how to build breathing room into a business.

**6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change**
Cut the lengthy ConvertKit quotes and instead use an example of a normal, non-digital business—like a local accounting firm or a wholesale bakery—to show exactly how "movement, loss, and concentration" apply to someone whose revenue doesn't come from automated monthly credit card subscriptions.

===== checks/readers/22-measuring-the-right-three-things/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

Halfway through I forgot this was meant to be for me. It's a long chapter about a bloke who sells email software to bloggers, and I run a café — well, I will do, in about six weeks. The bit that made me sit up was near the end: don't let the person who gets paid more when the number goes up be the one reading the number out. That's the only sentence I'd have written on my hand.

**2. What landed**

"A figure that needs you to explain it is one more thing that only you can do." That's the whole problem of me stepping back, put plainly. I've watched managers hand a staff member a takings sheet and the poor lad just nods.

"Losing five customers is one thing if they were the five smallest and another if one of them was the account that paid for the shop's rent." I know exactly who that is for me already — it'll be the two offices that order lunch in. And I like that the chapter says count customers lost *and* money lost as two separate numbers. Nobody's ever told me that; I'd have counted one.

"What counts as a lost one and on which day" — the definitions page, dated, changed only with a note saying when and why. That's the most useful practical instruction in the chapter and it costs nothing to do. Same with "A number nobody had to falsify. They only had to define it." I've seen that with wastage sheets. Nobody lied. The definition of wastage just got helpful.

And the last Monday item — after a quarter, write down what surprised you, and if none of the three moved first, they're the wrong three. That's the author admitting his own three might not be mine, and it made me trust him more, not less.

**3. What didn't**

Move 2 is four hundred words of somebody else's business and I nearly stopped. "Quick Ratio = (New MRR + Expansion MRR) / (Churned MRR + Contraction MRR)" — I don't need this, and the chapter tells me two pages later that I don't need it. Why put it in front of me at all? "Buffer at 1.08 and Hubstaff at 1.38" means nothing to me and reads like showing off.

"Net revenue against recurring is a distinction a subscription business needs and most of this book's readers do not." Good — but then take it out of the chapter, don't parade it and then excuse it.

And the concentration figure. My worry isn't three big customers, it's that half my trade is people walking past on a Tuesday and I won't know a single one of their names. "The share of the week's money from the top customers as you defined them in item one, with their names" — I can't. I'll have a card machine and a till. The chapter is honest that it's a compression of Barry's seven, but it never quite admits that a café has an anonymous till and no account names.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First time I thought the ConvertKit section was the chapter. Second time I saw it's just evidence, and the actual argument is Move 3, which is short and good. I also caught the November annual-plans bit properly — one decision, three numbers moving three ways, nobody lying. That's the point of the chapter and I sailed straight past it first time because I was drowning in MRR.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — I'd skim Move 2 and read the rest twice.

**6. One thing I'd change**

Give the three numbers once in cash-business terms before you give them in ConvertKit's — new against returning, customers lost and money lost, and for a walk-in trade something I can actually count as concentration (biggest day part? standing orders? the office accounts?). Then let Barry be the illustration instead of the entry.

===== checks/readers/22-measuring-the-right-three-things/claude-lagos.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

Halfway through I realised this whole chapter is built on one American software company's blog post from 2019, and I kept waiting for someone like me to show up. The core idea — pick three numbers, hand the reading to someone else, write the definitions down first — is genuinely useful and I can do it tomorrow. But "MRR", "expansion", "net revenue churn", "quick ratio" is a language I don't speak, and I had to translate every paragraph before I could use it.

**2. What landed**

"A figure that needs you to explain it is one more thing that only you can do." That's it exactly. My sister helps me pack orders and if I told her "we did ₦400k last week" she'd nod and it would mean nothing. That sentence named a problem I've had for a year without a word for it.

"Losing five customers is one thing if they were the five smallest and another if one of them was the account that paid for the shop's rent." I have three or four repeat buyers who order in bulk for their own resale. If one of them goes quiet I feel it in the same week. Counting customers lost and money lost as two separate numbers is obvious now and I wasn't doing it.

"A lost customer is lost on the day you wrote down in advance — thirty days without a return, sixty ... and not on a later day chosen once the number is in." This is the best line in the chapter. I would absolutely have moved that line. I've told myself "she'll order again next month" about a customer who stopped in February.

The S-1 anecdote — "A number nobody had to falsify. They only had to define it." — made the whole target/measure argument land in one sentence.

**3. What didn't**

"Each November ConvertKit pushes annual plans, sold at a discount: net revenue that month climbs 'as high as $3 million'..." Three paragraphs of a business at a million dollars a month explaining a subscription-discount calendar. I don't sell subscriptions. Nobody I know sells subscriptions. The chapter admits this late — "a distinction a subscription business needs and most of this book's readers do not" — but only after making me read all of it.

"Hand the reading to someone else ... the person should be someone the numbers do not pay." I'm one person with a sister who packs boxes and a tailor I pay per piece. Everyone near my business is paid by my business. The chapter treats this as a rule, not a difficulty, and gives me nothing when I can't meet it.

Also: not one word about inflation. My prices went up 40% this year and my "money in" grew while I sold fewer pieces. A movement number that doesn't separate price from volume will lie to me every single month, and the chapter never considers that a figure can rise for reasons that have nothing to do with the business.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

Second read I noticed the honesty — the chapter says out loud that three is *this book's* compression, that Barry describes seven, and that the evidence "reports no decision made on them and no surprise they missed." That admission bought back a lot of trust. I also saw Move 3 does give me the non-subscription version — "new customers this week against returning ones" — I'd just skimmed past it because it's one sentence buried after a wall of churn vocabulary.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — the Monday list alone is worth the next chapter.

**6. One concrete thing to change**

Give the three numbers in my language first, before ConvertKit: new buyers vs. repeat buyers, customers lost and naira lost, share of the week's money from the top three names. Then use Barry to test them. Right now I have to earn the chapter before it pays me.

===== checks/readers/22-measuring-the-right-three-things/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

The core idea is good: totals are lagging summaries, and definitions matter more than polished dashboards. But the chapter takes a long, SaaS-heavy route to arrive at something I’d summarize as “track movement, loss, and customer concentration—and define them before incentives corrupt them.” I also don’t quite believe these are literally three numbers.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“A figure that needs you to explain it is one more thing that only you can do” landed hardest. That’s exactly how founder dependency shows up in engineering and business operations: the dashboard exists, but the real interpretation layer is still one person’s undocumented context.

The annual-plan example was concrete and useful: “net revenue up, MRR down, churn up and then down again.” I’ve seen this kind of metric disagreement after pricing changes, migrations, and enterprise contract timing. It demonstrates why a metric can be calculated correctly and still produce a misleading story.

“A number nobody had to falsify. They only had to define it” is memorable without being cute. The advice to date definitions and change them publicly is operationally sound—basically version control for business semantics.

I also appreciated the admission that the chapter could be wrong: the three metrics might miss correlated customer risk or declining acquisition quality. Business books rarely specify their own failure condition.

### 3. What didn’t

I got bored during the long ConvertKit inventory: quick ratio, Buffer comparison, Hubstaff comparison, ARPU, customer split. Much of it is later discarded, so it reads like research notes the author couldn’t bear to cut.

The title promises three things, but “customers lost and money lost, as two numbers” already makes loss two things. Movement is also “new against returning,” concentration requires a percentage plus customer names, and cash remains weekly. In practice this is a compact dashboard, not three numbers. The forced neatness made me trust the prescription less.

The non-subscription accommodation—“the breakout is coarser and still exists”—is too brief. My hypothetical side business might involve projects, consulting, usage billing, or a marketplace, where weekly churn is meaningless or dangerously noisy.

I also doubt “the person should be someone the numbers do not pay.” In a two-to-five-person company, nearly everyone’s week improves when revenue, retention, or concentration improves. Independence sounds desirable, but the chapter doesn’t identify a realistic role.

Finally, “a person reading seven numbers aloud is reading none of them” talks down to the reader. Seven well-designed metrics are not inherently incomprehensible; the issue is whether they drive decisions.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I saw a good principle buried under an overlong case study. On the second, I noticed how carefully the author limits the claim: Barry did not endorse three metrics, the evidence is a founder post, cadence should follow the operating cycle, and quarterly surprises are explicitly used to invalidate the selection. Those caveats increased my trust, though they also weakened the confident “right three” framing.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes, because the author is unusually willing to state where the evidence ends.

### 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Replace half the ConvertKit walkthrough with one worked non-subscription example showing the exact three definitions, one Monday reading, and the decision each number triggered.

===== checks/readers/23-slack/agy-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
At first, it felt like yet another American tech-startup manual written by someone who has a safety net. As someone trying to build a modest, stable life in the Netherlands from scratch, the idea of deliberately keeping "slack" sounded like a dangerous luxury. But by the end, I realized it is actually a survival mechanism.

### 2. What landed
The concept of funding your safety margin safely spoke directly to my low tolerance for financial risk. The author writes: "Reserve is bought in units you can stop buying, funded from revenue that already repeats, and sized against a shock you have named." This is exactly how I have to operate; I cannot take out big loans or make bets on future demand. 

I also felt very seen by the line, "When the process breaks, you are the process." With family obligations and my life still in transition here, I am terrified of becoming trapped by my own business, where if I get sick, everything collapses. Recognizing that the owner is the default "unpriced" reserve is a painful but necessary truth.

### 3. What didn't
The entire WP Curve case study alienated me. The author describes it as "a flat-fee support business staffed largely in the Philippines." This is the exact kind of digital-nomad hustle optimism I distrust. I am a professional trying to rebuild a local accounting or marketing practice in Europe, not a tech founder exploiting cheap overseas labor so I can joke about the "4 hour work week" from a hammock. It felt out of touch with traditional, grounded businesses. 

Also, the advice in the Monday section to "Put the fortnight in the calendar this month... If the business cannot run two weeks without you, write down what broke" is ridiculous for someone in my position. I cannot deliberately break my income source just to test a theory.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I was so irritated by the tech-bro examples that I missed the underlying mechanics. On the second read, I stripped away the WP Curve story and looked only at the "Monday" checklist. I realized that writing down a process so someone else can do it isn't just about scaling a startup to millions; it's about making sure my business survives if I have to spend two weeks dealing with family emergencies or immigration paperwork. The core advice is actually very conservative.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, mainly to see if the financial advice in the next chapters remains risk-averse and practical.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Drop the WP Curve example and show how "slack" is built in an unglamorous, traditional small business—like a solo accounting firm or a local bakery—where you cannot just hire offshore freelancers for pennies to absorb your excess capacity.

===== checks/readers/23-slack/claude-cafe.md =====
**1. Gut reaction after first read**

The opening got me — "every hour sold, every person busy, the calendar full three weeks out and you proud of it" — that's the shift rota I've built a hundred times and been pleased with. Then it went off to a website company in Australia with fifteen developers in the Philippines and I stopped seeing myself in it. By the end I could tell there was a good idea buried in there but I'd had to dig for it with a spoon.

**2. What landed**

"You already know the failure from the inside, because you are the reserve. When someone is off, you cover. When the order doubles, you work the weekend." That's twenty-five years of my working life in three sentences and nobody's ever put it to me that way. I've always thought of it as being a good manager. It's not — it's the business having no other way to cope.

The waste-versus-reserve line: "An idle afternoon nobody owns is waste. The same afternoon, held so that the one person who knows the supplier can be away for a fortnight without the business stopping, is a reserve, and the difference is entirely in whether it was decided." That's clean. That I could explain to my sister.

And Monday item 5 — put a fortnight in the calendar and take it, write down what broke, in order, price each line. That's a real thing I could do that would tell me something I don't already know. I liked that the author admits he can't prove the chapter: "the honest guess at why it is hard to find is that a reserve which failed looks, afterwards, like a cost that should never have been carried." Nobody's tried to sell me anything there.

**3. What didn't**

The case study. Not one line of it is my world. "24 / 7 unlimited WordPress small fixes for $69 / month" — I had to read that twice to know what they sold. Then MRR, ARR, "$20,833 / month", "57 net customer increase", "another month of data from Hively". I don't know what Hively is and the author doesn't say. The author does eventually admit the numbers don't transfer — "a flat-fee support business staffed largely in the Philippines has a cost per person yours almost certainly does not" — but that's buried near the end of Move 3, after I've already waded through it.

And the prose fights me. "Reserve is bought in units you can stop buying, funded from revenue that already repeats, and sized against a shock you have named; capacity bought in any other way is a bet on demand, and chapter one already priced that." I know what it means now. I didn't the first time.

The chapter cross-references are a thicket — one, ten, fourteen, sixteen, eighteen, nineteen, twenty-two, twenty-eight. That paragraph beginning "Two other chapters own the edges" told me nothing except that other chapters exist.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

Second time I saw the actual argument, which is that the 82% happy score and the record revenue month are the same event and the company didn't notice. First read I skimmed past that as more numbers. It's the sharpest thing in the chapter and it's sitting in the middle of a paragraph, unlabelled. I also realised the "hire one more than the pace needs" instruction is the author's, not something the company did — the text does say so, but it takes two reads.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes, but I'd skim the case study, and I'd resent having to.

**6. One concrete thing to change**

Open Move 2 with one plain sentence — my chef is off for a month, or my Saturday doubles — before you go anywhere near WordPress and MRR. Give me my own version first, then use theirs as the evidence. As it stands you spend your best goodwill, earned in Move 1, on a company I can't picture.

===== checks/readers/23-slack/claude-lagos.md =====
# Report — Lagos, 24, clothing business on Instagram/WhatsApp

**1. Gut reaction after first read**

I understood the idea in the first two paragraphs and I liked it — hold back capacity on purpose, because right now the only spare capacity in my business is me, and I'm already spent. Then the whole middle became a company with fifteen staff and $23,535 a month, and I stopped being in the room. By the end I had one useful sentence and a lot of somebody else's monthly report.

**2. What landed**

"You already know the failure from the inside, because you are the reserve. When someone is off, you cover. When the order doubles, you work the weekend. When the process breaks, you are the process." That is my life exactly. Nobody has written it that plainly for me before — my tailor goes to the village for a burial, and there is no business until she comes back.

"An idle afternoon nobody owns is waste. The same afternoon, held so that the one person who knows the supplier can be away for a fortnight without the business stopping, is a reserve, and the difference is entirely in whether it was decided." That is a real distinction and I can use it tomorrow. My aunt would call my idle Tuesday laziness; now I can tell her what it's for.

"Reserve is bought in units you can stop buying" — this is the sentence I'd keep. In Lagos you never commit to anything you can't put down in a month. Inflation eats a fixed cost you signed in January by June.

Monday item 1 — the three sentences, "write what would break first" — costs nothing and I can do it on my phone tonight.

**3. What didn't**

The case. Four long paragraphs of "MRR grew by 22% to $23,535", "team to 15 people", "90% of the Xero bookkeeping", "hiring every 2 weeks". I don't have a team, an admin in the Philippines, Xero, affiliates, or a blog. The author sees this and half-admits it — "a flat-fee support business staffed largely in the Philippines has a cost per person yours almost certainly does not" — but that one line doesn't rescue three pages built on a company nothing like mine.

Monday item 5: "Put the fortnight in the calendar this month, with a date, and take it." Two weeks. Away. This month. My customers DM me at 11pm and buy from the next girl if I don't reply by morning. There is no version of my business where I disappear for fourteen days and come back to it. Give me the two-day version first and I'll believe you.

Monday item 3 offers "a contractor on a standing arrangement" — a standing arrangement means a retainer, and a retainer means cash I don't have, in a currency losing value while it sits. The chapter says reserve is funded "from revenue that already repeats." Mine doesn't repeat; it spikes at Christmas and Sallah and dies in between. Seasonal, not steady, is my whole shape, and it isn't here.

**4. What changed between read one and read two**

First read, I thought the case was the chapter. Second read, I saw the argument is actually in Move 3 and Monday, and the case is just illustration — and honestly it holds up better than I gave it credit for. I also caught "Reserve is spent silently" the second time, which I'd skimmed past: my December hires get eaten the same week I hire them. That's the truest thing here. And I noticed the author admits the case never shows a reserve being *tested* — I respect that, but it also means I'm reading three pages of a thing that proves nothing.

**5. Would I keep reading?**

Yes — the argument is real even when the example isn't mine.

**6. One concrete thing to change**

Replace half the WP Curve report with one owner-operator who has no staff: where does a one-person business hold reserve, and what does the drill look like at two days instead of two weeks?

===== checks/readers/23-slack/codex-dev.md =====
## 1. Gut reaction after first read

The core idea landed: if I’m the person absorbing every surprise, the business doesn’t actually have slack—it has an unpriced dependency. But the chapter takes a long, somewhat circular route to a fairly compact argument, and the WP Curve evidence doesn’t fully support the confidence of the prescription.

## 2. What landed — specific passages

“You are the reserve” is the sharpest line in the chapter. I’ve seen exactly this in engineering teams and in my own side-business experiments: the system appears efficient because one experienced person quietly handles escalations, weekends, and undocumented edge cases. Calling that capacity “unpriced, unsized, and spent first” makes the hidden cost concrete.

“Reserve is sized, named, and bought in units you can stop” also worked for me. That’s operationally useful. It separates deliberate headroom from vague overstaffing and addresses my immediate objection: unused salaried capacity can become an expensive permanent commitment.

The caveat that “the fifteen-to-$23,535 ratio…is not a benchmark for anything” earned credibility. Too many business books would turn one offshore WordPress support company’s staffing ratio into a universal formula.

The strongest practical exercise is the two-week absence drill: “write down what broke, in order, and put a monthly cost beside each line.” That produces evidence I could use to decide whether I need documentation, a contractor, cross-training, or actual additional capacity.

## 3. What didn’t

I got bored during the long reconstruction of WP Curve’s June 2014 post. Details such as affiliates, blog images, Xero bookkeeping, and the “4 hour work week, baby!” aside consume a lot of space without proving the central claim.

The chapter repeatedly implies a connection between rapid hiring and Happy % falling from 96% to 82%, then carefully admits the evidence does not establish one: it is “the only figure on the page that could have shown it, which is not the same as showing it.” That’s intellectually honest, but it leaves the case doing less work than the surrounding prose suggests. The decline might reflect request mix, measurement noise, customer selection, onboarding problems, or something else entirely.

“The reserve is the one unit beyond that pace” also feels asserted rather than derived. Why one person? A Bay Area engineer may cost $300K fully loaded; a contractor may take months to acquire system context. The right reserve depends on probability, recovery time, skill substitutability, margins, and contractual consequences—not merely one increment beyond current demand.

Finally, “take [the fortnight]” is unrealistic for many owner-operated businesses unless the chapter first distinguishes a controlled drill from abandoning customer obligations.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I thought the chapter was making a stronger empirical claim than it had earned. On the second, I noticed how explicitly it limits itself: “one month of reserve being built and no month of it being drawn on.” That restraint improved my view of the author, but also made the mismatch clearer—the case illustrates delegation and growth strain, not that the proposed reserve survives a shock.

## 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes, because the framework is useful enough to continue, though I’d want tighter evidence and fewer callbacks.

## 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Cut the WP Curve section by half and replace that space with a worked example calculating reserve options—employee, contractor, cross-training, or owner time—using cost, time-to-stop, recovery time, and the expected loss from a named shock.

