===== checks/readers/24-what-you-signed/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

I came away with one genuinely useful framework buried inside an overlong legal chapter: makers, customers, dependencies, and liability. It made me want to audit my hypothetical side business, but the extended Dutch-law block caused me to skim, and several universal-sounding claims felt stronger than the evidence presented.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“Nothing has gone wrong…is the only time this chapter is cheap” is sharp and credible. I’ve seen teams postpone ownership, vendor, and liability questions because production is working; once a relationship deteriorates, every missing document becomes leverage.

The best practical point is: “find the sentence that makes it yours.” That is specific enough to act on. Software people routinely confuse paying for development with owning the code, designs, domains, deployment accounts, and underlying licensed components.

“Move your terms from the invoice to the quote” also works. It converts an abstract enforceability warning into a concrete workflow change, including preserving the dated email as evidence.

Finally, I respected “What this chapter owes.” Admitting that no primary-document case was found—and labeling the ordering and thresholds as the book’s own rules of thumb—is unusually honest for a business book.

### 3. What didn’t

I checked out during the long Dutch statutory quotations beginning “The Auteurswet starts from the maker.” As a US reader, I understood why the jurisdiction-specific evidence was there, but several screens of untranslated law made me feel like an accidental visitor rather than the intended reader. A concise English explanation with the original language in notes would have been enough.

“Every jurisdiction has a version of the same test” and “Paying the invoice transfers nothing” sound too categorical. The chapter later admits that defaults vary, so these lines undermine its otherwise careful boundaries. In the US, I’d immediately want distinctions among copyright assignment, work made for hire, implied license, ownership of physical deliverables, and third-party components.

“A professional’s hour is owed when…” is appealingly concrete but commercially naive. A competent attorney may need onboarding, document review, conflicts checks, and follow-up; “one hour” risks becoming another reassuring slogan. The triggers also ignore cumulative exposure from many small recurring transactions.

I also don’t buy the fixed ordering that puts liability last because it is “least likely.” For food, healthcare, security, infrastructure, or professional advice, catastrophic liability may deserve first position.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the second read, I noticed how disciplined the chapter is about separating its sourced Dutch claims from its unsourced heuristics. That raised my trust. But I also noticed that the strongest, most memorable universal claims are precisely where the sourcing is weakest, so my confidence in the four-part checklist increased while my confidence in its ordering and thresholds decreased.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the liability chapter is the obvious test of whether this framework becomes operational or remains cautionary prose.

### 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Cut the Dutch-law box by two-thirds and use that space for one fully worked US-style example showing the actual documents, disputed clause, dollars at risk, and what the lawyer changed.

===== checks/readers/24-what-you-signed/flash-cafe.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It gave me a proper jolt of panic about my café logo and lease before hitting me with a wall of Dutch legal text. It is blunt, refreshingly free of motivational TED-talk fluff, and understands why people rely on handshakes. But halfway through, I wondered why on earth a woman opening a café in the north of England was being handed pages of Dutch statute.

### 2. What landed
- *"The instinct that keeps them unwritten is not laziness. It is that writing them down feels like the moment the business stops being a relationship..."* That is 25 years of hospitality in a nutshell; you survive on goodwill with local suppliers and regulars, so putting legal paper between you feels distrustful.
- *"Paying the invoice transfers nothing."* This stopped me dead. I paid a local lad to design my café logo, window vinyls, and menu boards; I assumed paying the bank transfer meant I owned them outright.
- *"A professional's hour is owed when the agreement carries more than a month's revenue, lasts longer than a year, contains the words unlimited, indemnify or exclusive..."* When you are watching every penny of redundancy money and a bank loan, having a clear rule for when to spend £200 on a solicitor is invaluable.

### 3. What didn't
- The raw Dutch law box: *“Het auteursrecht is het uitsluitend recht van den maker van een werk...”* Being told to "skip to Move 3" if I'm not in the Netherlands felt lazy. If I bought this book in the UK, dumping foreign statutes on me feels alienating and irrelevant.
- The consultancy jargon: Calling sections *"Move 1"*, *"Move 2"*, and *"Move 3"* feels like business-school posturing.
- The lack of fit for counter-service trade: *“Move your terms from the invoice to the quote. Attach them to whatever the customer sees before they say yes...”* In a café, people buy a toastie and a coffee at the till. We don’t issue quotes or invoices to customers, so this advice completely ignores over-the-counter retail and hospitality.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first pass, the author’s stern tone made me feel reckless and behind on my paperwork. On the second read, I spotted the admission under *Note*: *“No company case: none was found with a primary document showing a cost that followed from an unwritten or undelivered agreement.”* The author sounds completely certain of impending doom, yet admits they have no documented real-world case of a small business actually ruined by this. The "Monday" action list is solid, but the theoretical scare-mongering is heavier than the evidence.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because despite the Dutch detour and office-job bias, it cuts through the usual romantic nonsense and tells me straight where my money is exposed before I open the doors.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Move the foreign statutory quotes into an appendix and replace them with plain-English examples that apply to physical, cash-over-the-counter businesses rather than just digital agencies and freelancers.

===== checks/readers/24-what-you-signed/flash-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
This gave me a sharp spike of anxiety, but it felt grounding rather than overwhelming. Having had my life upended once, I am deeply afraid of hidden legal traps destroying the modest savings my family has here in the Netherlands. I was intensely relieved to find zero American-style hustle cheerleading, but my immediate dread was whether I can actually afford to fix these gaps.

### 2. What landed
* **The copyright default:** *"Paying the invoice transfers nothing."* Coming from an accounting/marketing background, I assumed payment implied transfer of rights. Learning that under the Dutch *Auteurswet* (Article 2, lid 3), hiring a freelance web designer on a tight budget leaves intellectual property with them unless transferred in writing is critical. It protects me from a catastrophic mistake before spending money I cannot afford to lose.
* **The timing of terms:** *"A link in the footer of the invoice, sent after the work was ordered, is the commonest way for a business to have terms it cannot hold anyone to."* This is practical, clear, and explains the Dutch *ter hand gesteld* rule (BW 6:234) without academic fluff. 
* **The tone on relationships:** *"The instinct that keeps them unwritten is not laziness. It is that writing them down feels like the moment the business stops being a relationship..."* The author understands why people avoid contracts without mocking them.

### 3. What didn't
* **The casual cost of legal advice:** *"Buy one hour. Take items 1 to 4 and the two agreements that carry the most money to a lawyer..."* In the Netherlands, an *advocaat* or specialized jurist easily charges €250–€350 per hour. When you live under temporary protection with finite savings and family obligations, telling me to "buy an hour" as a casual Monday task feels out of touch with low-capital, self-funded micro-businesses.
* **Raw Dutch statute barriers:** Dropping dense, archaic legal Dutch like *"tenzij tusschen partijen anders is overeengekomen"* without a parallel plain-English gloss. My Dutch is improving, but navigating formal statutory language on liability and IP while evaluating business viability felt alienating.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On read one, I felt paralyzed by the Dutch box and assumed I needed hundreds of euros for a lawyer immediately before doing anything. On read two, I noticed the filtering logic in *When to pay someone who knows*: an hour is only owed if an agreement *"carries more than a month's revenue, lasts longer than a year, contains the words unlimited..."* That distinction actually gave me permission to rely on carefully handed-over templates for small jobs and keep my cash intact.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because it respects my intelligence and treats business as risk containment rather than an optimistic fantasy.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Provide a two-line plain English paraphrase alongside the Dutch statute quotes, and suggest lower-cost entry points (such as KvK standard contract formats, legal expense insurance/*rechtsbijstand*, or jurist consults) before telling a fledgling founder to simply hire a lawyer.

===== checks/readers/24-what-you-signed/pro-lagos.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
I felt a mix of anxiety and exhaustion. The core message hit hard—that unwritten rules will bite me eventually—but then the chapter swerved into a massive block of foreign legal jargon that made my eyes glaze over. It felt like another business book written for someone in a stable country with a corporate lawyer on speed dial, not someone selling clothes via WhatsApp in Lagos where "the law's default" is basically whoever shouts the loudest. 

### 2. What landed — specific passages (quote briefly) and why they worked for someone like me
The absolute truth of this line got me: *"An agreement you did not write down is not an agreement without terms. Its terms are the law's default plus the other side's memory, and they are read out on the day of the dispute by whoever has the most to gain from their version."* 

That is exactly how survival works here. When a dispatch rider ruins a delivery or a tailor messes up a batch of fabric, it always turns into a shouting match based on "memory." I also loved the advice to move terms *"from the invoice to the quote."* I usually send my no-refund policy after they've paid the deposit, and customers always fight me on it. Moving it upfront costs me zero naira and actually makes practical sense for my WhatsApp hustle.

### 3. What didn't — where I got bored, doubted the author, felt excluded or talked down to, or felt the advice didn't fit my situation
I completely checked out during the giant grey box about "Dutch ground." Quoting the *"Auteurswet"* in actual Dutch? Come on. I’m hustling on IG with terrible electricity and inflation eating my margins; I do not have the patience for European copyright law. 

I also felt totally excluded when the author said: *"Take the sentence, the number and your legal form to an insurance adviser and ask what a policy against it costs."* An insurance adviser? For an IG clothing vendor in Nigeria? If a batch of clothes gets ruined, I eat the cost or fight the supplier. Small business insurance for this level of trade essentially doesn't exist here, and pretending everyone can just *"buy one hour"* with a lawyer ignores how prohibitively expensive and intimidating that is when you are strictly cash-constrained.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, the Dutch legal text made me want to close the book, and I almost dismissed the whole chapter as Eurocentric garbage. On the second read, I skipped the legal box entirely and realized the underlying framework—categorizing risks into makers, customers, platforms, and liability—is actually brilliant. I realized the author was just using the Dutch law as a placeholder to prove a point: that default laws exist, even if you don't know them. The Monday action plan at the end is actually something I can execute this week from my phone, even without a lawyer.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter? 
Yes, because even though it assumes I have structural resources I don't, the raw logic about protecting my business from bad memories is too valuable to ignore.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Cut the untranslated Dutch legal text completely and replace it with a simple, universal story of how a default law destroyed a small business; right now, that giant box is a massive roadblock that makes impatient readers like me want to quit.

===== checks/readers/25-what-protects-you/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

The core test—what protection actually does when the feared event occurs—is sharp and useful. But the chapter spends too long restating it, while relying on one founder anecdote that explicitly does not reveal whether the decision worked.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“Write the event, not the fear” landed immediately. As an engineer, I can work with a concrete threat model—who does what with which asset—far better than with generalized founder anxiety.

The distinction between buying protection and buying “standing” was also valuable: “the protection is the process, priced separately and paid later.” I’ve signed NDAs and reviewed IP clauses, but I haven’t always mentally attached enforcement cost, management distraction, discovery, and the chance of collecting anything. Writing the second price beside the filing or contract price is a genuinely useful move.

I also trusted the author more for admitting, “Studio Neat’s post is not that case, and it is not the opposite either.” That is unusually honest for a business book. The evidence shows the decision, not the outcome.

### 3. What didn’t

The claim that “A copy takes the customers who were never choosing you” is much too confident. A well-funded competitor can copy the product, bundle it, out-distribute me, undercut me, and take customers who previously chose me quite deliberately. In software, switching costs, integrations, procurement relationships, platform access, and sales reach can matter more than whether customers liked my email response.

The follow-on assertion—“at this scale it rarely is” in the object alone—also feels unsupported. For a small SaaS company, the object may include years of specialized engineering, data, workflow design, or technical performance. “Ship the next version” is not necessarily a defense against a larger company with more engineers and an existing channel.

I got bored during the repeated explanations of patents as permission to sue. Studio Neat’s quotes make the point once; the chapter then makes it several more times. Meanwhile, patents’ possible value in fundraising, acquisition diligence, deterrence, defensive portfolios, or licensing is barely considered.

Monday item 3 sounds empirical but asks for a counterfactual the records cannot answer. Returning-versus-new revenue does not tell me which customers a future copy would take.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I thought the chapter was basically anti-patent with disclaimers. On the second, I noticed it is more disciplined than that: it repeatedly limits the evidence, distinguishes jurisdictions, and tells me to consult an expert. I also noticed that its strongest idea is threat modeling and full-cost accounting, while its weakest idea is presenting customer loyalty as the default small-business moat.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes, because the author’s evidentiary honesty outweighs the repetition and overreach.

### 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Replace one-third of the Studio Neat repetition with a contrasting case where a small company enforced—or regretted not obtaining—IP protection, including actual legal cost and business outcome.

===== checks/readers/25-what-protects-you/flash-cafe.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It’s much more down-to-earth than I expected from a book that chops things into "Moves," though it still smells faintly of an American tech seminar. The core point hit me like cold water: buying a fancy legal document doesn't stop bad luck; it just buys you an expensive ticket to a courtroom you can't afford. For someone putting her redundancy money and a bank loan on the line for a café, it was a massive relief to hear that I don't need to throw thousands at lawyers just to feel safe.

### 2. What landed
* **"the filing does not stop the copy; it lets you hire a lawyer to argue about the copy."** 
* **"On the morning the person you trained opens the list at a competitor's desk, what does the non-disclosure agreement do — stop the opening, or give you a claim about it?"**
* **"...the one kind of protection at this scale that has to be earned rather than bought — being the one the customers choose, and come back to."**

In 25 years running dining rooms, I’ve seen head chefs walk out on a Friday afternoon with half the kitchen staff and the house recipes. A non-disclosure agreement in a drawer doesn't cook the Saturday night service. Real protection on a high street isn't paper; it’s regular customers who come back because the coffee is hot, the staff are warm, and the food is consistent.

### 3. What didn't
* **"From chapter eleven's renew-and-reorder column and chapter twenty-two's returning-against-new figure, with the concentration figure's names beside them..."**
* **"...a small moulded clamp that reached its funding goal eighty-five minutes after one link in October 2010..."**

All the constant cross-referencing to previous chapters and spreadsheet columns made my eyes glaze over—I'm opening a café, not sitting an accounting exam. Basing the whole argument on two American lads selling plastic phone clamps on Kickstarter felt miles away from my reality. Selling plastic bits over the internet to Germany isn't the same as dealing with local footfall, supplier bills, and council rates.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I skimmed right past the section titled *What would prove this chapter wrong*, thinking it was academic padding. On the second read, that part actually won me over. The author admits they don't know if Studio Neat’s brand actually survived the knockoffs long-term, and confesses they couldn't find a small-business case study for insurance claims. Most business gurus pretend they have a flawless formula; admitting what the evidence doesn't prove made the author feel honest rather than preachy.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because despite the annoying spreadsheet talk, it respects my intelligence and might stop me blowing my savings on useless paperwork.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Drop the fixation on online gadget startups and give at least one grounded, physical example—like a bakery, a mechanic, or a café—so regular people risking their life savings on the high street don't feel like this book was only written for tech kids.

===== checks/readers/25-what-protects-you/flash-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
Coming from Ukraine, where small entrepreneurs know the legal system rarely shields the vulnerable, this felt brutally accurate and surprisingly relieving. It confirmed my deep suspicion that spending my limited savings on formal legal paperwork would just buy an illusion of security. However, it also sparked anxiety: if my only real moat is out-running copycats with constant new output, that assumes a buffer of time and capital I simply do not have while rebuilding my life in the Netherlands.

### 2. What landed
The sharp distinction between genuine protection and unbudgeted litigation landed hard: *"Some give you the right to start an expensive process you have not budgeted for."* With my family depending on every euro and my status under temporary protection, hiring a Dutch attorney to enforce a clause is out of the question. Recognizing that an instrument often only buys *"standing, and the protection is the process, priced separately and paid later"* gives me permission not to waste scarce cash on legal theatre. The line *"the first figure alone is what the comfort costs"* accurately diagnoses how fear manipulates founders into buying peace of mind instead of solvency.

### 3. What didn't
The casual attitude of the case study irritated me: *"There is not much we can do, other than shrug our shoulders and carry on."* That shrug belongs to comfortable American founders with safety nets, not an immigrant with zero margin for failure. Furthermore, the brief aside—*"A reader in the Netherlands or anywhere in Europe holds different instruments from the two the post names"*—felt like an afterthought. Mentioning Europe without engaging with how Dutch regulatory realities, KvK registrations, or local liability actually function made me feel like an outsider eavesdropping on an American conversation.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I was annoyed by the Studio Neat narrative, viewing it as another piece of Silicon Valley hustle optimism telling people to just work harder. On the second read, I noticed the author's own skepticism in *What would prove this chapter wrong*. The author explicitly admits Studio Neat's choice was merely a *"bet placed, with no report of how it paid"* and points out the missing evidence regarding insurance. Seeing the author critique their own evidence made the chapter feel like rigorous accounting rather than startup cheerleading.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because unlike most business writing, it treats cash as strictly finite and refuses to sell comfortable illusions to people who cannot afford them.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Replace or balance the American Kickstarter gadget example with a modest European service or B2B example, where someone facing an ex-employee taking a client list cannot simply "design more great products" to survive.

===== checks/readers/25-what-protects-you/pro-lagos.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
I rolled my eyes at first because it sounded like more Silicon Valley tech-bro theory about patents and Kickstarters. But once it stripped away the legal illusion and said protection is just the right to spend money on lawyers, it hit home hard. For someone hustling on Instagram in Lagos where the courts take a decade, accepting that outrunning the copycats is my only real defense is actually a huge relief.

### 2. What landed — specific passages and why they worked for someone like me.
The realization that legal instruments don't stop theft, they just "give you the right to start an expensive process you have not budgeted for." That is the absolute truth in Nigeria. If a local supplier steals my dress design, suing them is a joke—I don't have the cash, and our justice system is notoriously slow. Also, the line, "A copy takes the customers who were never choosing you." I felt that in my chest. Let the bargain hunters buy the cheap knockoffs; my real WhatsApp list buys from me for my styling, my customer service, and the fact that my dispatch rider actually shows up on time. 

### 3. What didn't — where I got bored, doubted the author, felt excluded or talked down to, or felt the advice didn't fit my situation.
I got irritated when the book leaned on "Studio Neat" and their Kickstarter hitting its goal in "eighty-five minutes." That frictionless world doesn't exist for me; I'm fighting crazy inflation and fluctuating exchange rates just to import fabric. Also, the Monday action item to "Ask an expert once... in the hour chapter twenty-four told you to buy." Please. I am bootstrapping this from my Infinix phone. I am not paying a Nigerian lawyer's consultation fee just to be told I can't afford to enforce a non-disclosure agreement against my former assistant. It assumes a level of institutional trust and spare cash I simply don't have.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two.
On the first read, I was so distracted by the American context (patents, Kickstarters, US dollars) that I almost wrote the whole chapter off as irrelevant. On the second read, I realized the author is actually validating my reality. The book is actively telling me *not* to waste my scarce capital on legal illusions and to pour it back into my next clothing drop instead ("putting money into the thing that cannot be copied"). It confirmed my natural survival instinct: just keep moving faster than the thieves.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because if chapter 26 is about "When the Ground Moves," I want to see if the author actually understands what it takes to survive when your economy's ground is constantly shaking.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change.
Stop using software patents and Kickstarter tech-bros to explain basic business survival; give an example of a regular retail or service business getting ripped off, because we are the ones who desperately need to hear that we shouldn't blow our last funds on useless exclusivity clauses.

