===== checks/readers/26-when-the-ground-moves/codex-dev.md =====
## 1. Gut reaction after first read

The central reframing—an external shock happens to customers first, then propagates into your revenue—felt useful and immediately applicable. But the chapter stretches one founder’s retrospective into fairly confident operating advice, especially around delaying cuts, without enough evidence to justify that confidence.

## 2. What landed — specific passages

“What can our customers no longer do, what can they still do, and what do they now need” is the strongest passage. As an engineer, I recognize this as debugging the upstream state change instead of blindly tuning the component showing the symptom. It also maps cleanly to a SaaS side business: interview customers about changed workflows before discounting or shipping the roadmap faster.

“Freeze before cancel” is concrete and commercially smart. A dated, reversible pause preserves the relationship without pretending current usage will recover immediately. I could actually implement that in billing and measure reactivation.

I also respected the caveat that Gymdesk’s recovery is “reported, not explained.” The author explicitly admits that gyms reopened independently, the churn rate is missing, and this is survivorship-biased founder testimony. That restraint earned more trust than the success story itself.

Finally, “the workaround with money in it” is memorable. Asking what customers did last week and what the workaround costs is much better than asking hypothetical willingness-to-pay questions.

## 3. What didn’t

“The cut, if it comes, comes on that date on the reading, not in week one on the fear” sounds cleaner than reality. Customer discovery, product adaptation, and reversible cost controls can run concurrently. Waiting until a calculated runway date may make later cuts deeper, especially when churn is still developing and revenue lags the shock.

“Build the smallest thing in the third column, within a month” also overreaches. Five interviews and a populated column do not establish that the proposed feature will retain customers or generate revenue. Gymdesk’s capacity limits and online training are plausible, but the chapter supplies no adoption, retention, or feature-level revenue data.

I got bored during the repeated qualifications about what the founder’s account does and does not prove. The intellectual honesty is good, but the same limitation is stated several times.

The chronology weakened my confidence: the vaccination-check feature is acknowledged as not being from 2020, while vaccines are described as “starting to become available” as 2021 closed. That timeline feels muddled and makes the case harder to audit.

The many references to earlier chapters also made the advice feel dependent on a private vocabulary I had to reconstruct.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the first read, I mainly retained the customer-first framing and the pause mechanism. On the second, I noticed how much of the prescriptive framework—the ordering of discovery before cuts, proactive freezes, and the one-month build deadline—is the book’s invention rather than something demonstrated by Gymdesk. I also appreciated more that the author labels that boundary, even while occasionally writing past it.

## 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes, because the framework is useful enough to continue, though I’d keep separating evidence from the author’s extrapolation.

## 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Replace the blanket “cuts wait” rule with a decision table covering runway, shock reversibility, leading indicators, and reversible versus irreversible cuts, then show how Gymdesk’s known numbers would populate it.

===== checks/readers/26-when-the-ground-moves/flash-cafe.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
I was ready to roll my eyes at another know-it-all manual, but that opening hit me right in the chest because I’ve lived through roadworks choking off trade before. The core point—stop panicking, don't immediately slash your staff or prices, and look at what your customers are actually dealing with—is bloody good common sense. But all the tech-firm acronyms nearly lost me halfway through, and it felt like a book written for people sitting behind laptops rather than someone wiping down tables and watching the till.

### 2. What landed
* **The opening diagnosis:** *"The road outside is dug up for a year... The shock is not yours, and the first mistake is to treat it as if it were"* rang completely true. In hospitality, when takings plummet from something outside, bad owners blame the chef or the waiting staff instead of looking out the front window.
* **Holding your nerve on cuts:** *"The cut, if it comes, comes on that date on the reading, not in week one on the fear."* That’s proper management. Knee-jerk panic—sacking your best barista or cheapening your ingredients the second trade dips—ruins a place before the storm even passes.
* **The honesty about survival:** Acknowledging that *"the vendor to the same gyms that did not survive has left no post"* made me trust the author. Most business books pretend their little frameworks guarantee you’ll survive, but this admitted you can do everything right and still go under if the road stays shut.

### 3. What didn't
* **Tech jargon and irrelevant models:** Stuff like *"consistent $1k+ MRR growth"* and *"churn from March to around August"* made my eyes glaze over. A one-man software outfit with no payroll or stock has nothing in common with a high-street café risking a bank loan.
* **Dismissive hand-waving at real businesses:** Saying *"the café whose office customers are at home may find they still eat lunch... here the book is guessing on your behalf"* felt lazy. Don't spend five pages pulling apart gym software and then give me a half-baked guess about the trade I actually work in.
* **Impractical advice for foot-traffic retail:** Telling me to *"Offer the pause before it is asked for"* makes sense for gym memberships or software subs, but you cannot put a flat white or a bacon sandwich on a "dated freeze." 
* **Constant homework references:** Being told every two paragraphs to check *"chapter twenty-two"*, *"chapter twenty-three"*, or *"chapter eleven's columns"* felt exhausting and patronising.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I assumed the author was holding up this gym software founder as some heroic genius we're all meant to copy. On the second read, I realized the author was actually skeptical of him—pointing out that the fellow's timeline was foggy, his vaccine check feature couldn't have existed in 2020, and there’s no proof his new features actually saved the business rather than gyms just reopening anyway. 

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Aye, I would, because despite the annoying tech examples and endless cross-referencing, there is a rare, no-nonsense honesty here that doesn't treat business like a fairy tale.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Stop leaning entirely on one-man software companies to prove your points, and give a proper, worked-out case of a physical high-street shop or kitchen dealing with actual rent, perishable stock, and wages.

===== checks/readers/26-when-the-ground-moves/flash-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
When I saw the title "When the Ground Moves," my mind went straight to February 2022, so reading about a software founder's gym clients closing in 2020 felt almost trivial at first. However, the tone surprised me: it was calm, cold, and completely free of the toxic "hustle and pivot" cheerleading I usually hate in business books. It treated external crisis as a concrete operational problem rather than an inspirational test of character.

### 2. What landed
The sober financial discipline resonated with my background and my current need to protect every euro.
- *"The cut, if it comes, comes on that date on the reading, not in week one on the fear."* Having lived through sudden upheaval, panic makes you want to freeze or slash everything immediately; anchoring cost cuts to a calculated runway date offers real, steadying control.
- *"Take chapter thirteen's cash — the bank balance less what is held for the tax authority..."* Coming from an accounting background and navigating the Dutch system (*Belastingdienst*), I appreciated that it explicitly separates tax obligations from usable cash before calculating survival time.
- *"...the vendor to the same gyms that did not survive has left no post."* The author’s direct admission of survivorship bias made me trust them; they aren't pretending this case is a guaranteed formula.

### 3. What didn't
The reliance on a solo software developer makes the advice feel detached from the reality of running a low-margin, physical small business.
- *"...there was, by the same document, almost nothing to cut... Gymdesk had no reserve of either kind to hold in 2020 except its owner, and the post shows that one being spent..."* For someone with family obligations, limited savings, and temporary legal status in the Netherlands, "spending the owner" is not a viable strategy. Burning through personal health and time with no margin for error is a luxury for young solo founders without dependents.
- *"The same move should exist for a business with no software in it, and here the book is guessing on your behalf: the café whose office customers are at home may find they still eat lunch..."* Admitting the book is "guessing" here felt dismissive. A physical business has fixed Dutch commercial rent, inventory waste, and strict local municipal regulations—you cannot simply reconfigure lunch service overnight the way a developer writes code for capacity limits.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On my first read, I felt alienated by another tech-founder case study and assumed the author was glorifying a standard startup survival story. On the second read, I realized the author is actually stripping away the founder's self-congratulatory narrative, repeatedly noting that the evidence is weak and that the only thing worth keeping is the disciplined sequence: observe the customer, offer a pause, check the cash runway, and decide without panic.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because its strict, unromantic method for measuring financial runway respects my low tolerance for risk far more than standard business advice.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Replace the speculative café and tradesman examples with a real, documented case of a non-software, low-margin business managing a shock, including actual cash numbers rather than admitting the book is guessing.

===== checks/readers/26-when-the-ground-moves/pro-lagos.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It sounds like a smart American talking about a tech company with a financial cushion I can only dream of. The core idea makes sense—don't panic, figure out what my customers can still afford—but the example of a software guy taking months to adapt feels like a completely different universe. When the economy crashes here in Lagos, it crashes today, and I don't have months of cash runway to sit back and observe.

### 2. What landed
The idea of looking at what the customer is going through instead of just my own panic. *"The shock is not yours... It is something that happened to your customers, and it reaches you through them."* This hit hard. When fuel prices tripled recently, people stopped buying my two-piece sets not because my clothes got worse, but because transport ate their disposable income. The advice to ask what they *now* need—which for me meant pushing cheaper loungewear instead of expensive party dresses—is exactly how I survive inflation spikes.

### 3. What didn't
The luxury of waiting to react. I got irritated here: *"The cut, if it comes, comes on that date on the reading, not in week one on the fear."* My brother, if my dispatch rider doubles his fee on Monday because of fuel scarcity, I have to adjust my prices or cut my own costs by Monday afternoon. I don't have a *"bank balance less what is held for the tax authority"* that covers me for months. I have the working capital sitting in my OPay account. I also felt excluded by the subscription-business framing; offering a *"pause — dated, cheap, easy to reverse"* means nothing to me. You can't pause buying a dress; you just ghost my WhatsApp.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I was just annoyed by the Gymdesk example because it felt entirely disconnected from hustling physical goods on IG. On the second read, I forced myself to strip away the software jargon and realized the underlying framework is actually solid. I just had to mentally translate "build a new software feature" into "source a more affordable fabric," and "pause an account" into "keep checking in on them via WhatsApp status even if they aren't buying this month to keep the relationship alive."

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because once you translate the Silicon Valley speak, the logic underneath is cold, clear, and completely free of the usual motivational nonsense.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Stop relying solely on a B2B subscription software example; give me just one paragraph showing how this framework applies to a transactional, cash-based business selling physical goods, because right now it assumes every reader has recurring revenue and months of runway.

===== checks/readers/27-built-to-leave/codex-dev.md =====
## 1. Gut reaction after first read

The core test—could a stranger run and understand this business without me?—is sharp and immediately relevant to someone like me who has considered turning consulting or a SaaS side project into something sellable. But the Black Hops story felt longer and less conclusive than the lesson it was carrying.

## 2. What landed — specific passages

“What is written down, priced, and legible without you in the room” is the strongest line. In engineering terms, that distinguishes a system from a heroic maintainer: documentation matters, but so do observable numbers and explicit operating decisions.

“The failure is building the first while believing you are building the second” also worked. The practice-versus-asset distinction cuts through the usual business-book promise that every owner should build a scalable company. A lucrative practice may be exactly what I want; I just should not assign it a software-company valuation in my head.

The most useful exercise was giving the accounts to an experienced outsider “without asking me anything.” That is concrete, falsifiable, and uncomfortable in a productive way. I could actually do it.

I also respected the sentence, “The transfer was clean. What transferred was smaller than what had been priced.” It captures how operational independence and business quality are separate questions.

## 3. What didn’t

The chapter leans too hard on Black Hops without establishing the outcome that matters. It explicitly admits that “nothing here shows whether the business the manager took over survived being handed to him.” That leaves me with evidence of weak financial visibility and questionable crowdfunding valuation, not evidence that the business was or was not built to leave.

The narration of dates, titles, prior chapters, executive résumés, and competing profit measures became procedural. By “Dan Norris has been in this book three times,” I felt I was being asked to manage the book’s internal continuity instead of learning the principle.

Some advice is stated more confidently than the evidence supports: “If the list has one entry, or none, you have been absorbing costs.” Not necessarily. Automation, purchasing leverage, product mix, or deliberately lower margins could explain stable prices. Tell me to inspect gross margin and unit economics; do not infer the answer from price-change count alone.

The “What would prove this chapter wrong” section also rigs the test. A documented, founder-independent business can fail after transfer for dozens of reasons unrelated to hidden founder relationships. The proposed counterexample is too narrow to falsify the broader claim.

## 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the second read, I appreciated that the author carefully avoids claiming Norris’s departure caused the loss and openly states the case’s limitations. I also noticed, though, that these caveats weaken the case more than the confident framing acknowledges. The chapter’s best argument is conceptual; the featured evidence mostly proves that outsiders can uncover accounting and pricing problems.

## 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes—the practice-versus-asset distinction is useful enough that I would read the final chapter.

## 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Cut the Black Hops narrative by a third and use that space for a worked example of an owner’s one-page handover, including the exact financial metrics an outsider should be able to reconstruct.

===== checks/readers/27-built-to-leave/flash-cafe.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
I nearly shut the thing the minute it started wittering on about sixty-seven-million-dollar valuations and equity crowd-funding, because none of that has anything to do with opening a small café with redundancy money. But that opening bit about who takes over when you're ill stopped me cold—in twenty-five years of hospitality, I’ve seen too many places collapse the second the head chef or owner ended up in hospital with all the supplier knowledge locked in their head. It felt stiff and over-intellectual, but it struck an uncomfortable nerve.

### 2. What landed
* **The illness reality check:** *"whoever is standing there when you are ill — and on that day the business will be worth exactly what a stranger can run and read."* Having managed kitchens and front-of-house for decades, I know the panic when a manager goes down and nobody knows the alarm code, the prep quantities, or which dairy supplier does the Thursday delivery.
* **The pricing cowardice:** *"The business in six years just had one price increase."* and *"you have been absorbing costs... and the stranger will find it in the margin before you do."* That is the single biggest trap in food service; owners get scared of upsetting the regulars by adding 20p to a flat white while electric bills and bacon prices shoot through the roof.
* **The personality trap:** *"The beer transfers; the three blokes do not"*. If my café only works because I'm behind the counter charming the locals, I haven't built a viable trade; I've just bought myself an exhausting job that'll die when my knees give out.

### 3. What didn't
* **The startup jargon:** *"raised more than $2.2 million from 996 investors in a day at a valuation of more than $67 million... with an EBITDA the year before of $1.76 million."* That sounds like Silicon Valley nonsense or a TED talk; I don't care about corporate regulatory filings when I'm trying to figure out commercial kitchen lease terms and VAT thresholds.
* **The constant syllabus homework:** *"the last three years of chapter twenty-two's figures, the price history, chapter twenty-four's four agreements, chapter twenty-three's reserve count..."* It feels like being scolded by a schoolmaster for not having your study binder in order, which makes someone like me who hates paperwork want to throw the book across the room.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I felt defensive and annoyed, assuming the author was just another consultant writing for tech firms and corporate buyouts. On the second pass, I noticed where they admitted *"The limit of the case is its size"* and explicitly validated running a "practice" that just provides a solid, honest living rather than a sellable empire. The "Monday" instruction to hand my numbers to the shopkeeper two doors down to see what they can't make sense of went from sounding like an academic exam to a dead-simple, practical test I could actually do.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because despite the boardroom jargon, the underlying warning about not burying your head in the sand is too sharp to ignore when you're putting your life savings on the line.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Swap the multi-million-pound Australian brewery story for a normal high-street baker or sandwich shop, and strip out the endless references to past chapter numbers so it reads like straight advice rather than a training manual.

===== checks/readers/27-built-to-leave/flash-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
This felt surprisingly grounded compared to typical startup advice, avoiding empty hype and focusing on cold documentation. Having had to abandon my career in Ukraine in days, the emphasis on being prepared for sudden absence resonated immediately. However, reading about a $67-million Australian craft brewery felt foreign and disconnected from my reality of trying to start a modest freelance service in the Netherlands with limited savings.

### 2. What landed
* **"every reason you might leave arrives unannounced"** — This struck me deeply. In 2022, I learned firsthand that stability vanishes overnight; ensuring systems can function if you are suddenly gone is not an abstract corporate exercise, but basic resilience.
* **"there are two legitimate things to build: a practice that gives you good work and a good living, and an asset that runs and can be owned by others. Neither is wrong."** — As someone rebuilding under temporary protection with family obligations, I appreciated the explicit validation of building a modest, stable livelihood rather than being pressured into high-risk scaling.
* **"The business in six years just had one price increase."** — From my background in finance and accounting, this specific failure mechanism rang completely true: absorbing rising costs silently out of fear or negligence will erode margins until the business collapses.

### 3. What didn't
* **"raised more than $2.2 million from 996 investors in a day at a valuation of more than $67 million"** — The casual ease of massive hype funding felt alienating. For someone with low capital and no safety net, these numbers feel like Silicon Valley fantasy rather than practical business reality.
* **"Give the monthly page and the last two years' accounts to one person who has run a business... your accountant, or the owner of the shop two doors down"** — This assumes an effortless local network. As a newcomer still learning Dutch and navigating local business culture, I don't have established local peers or a friendly neighboring shopkeeper to review my confidential accounts.
* The constant shorthand callbacks (*"chapter twenty-two's figures", "chapter sixteen's number", "chapter twenty-four's four agreements"*) felt frustratingly opaque without brief contextual cues.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I was distracted by the sheer scale of the brewery and the drama of founders stepping down. On the second read, the underlying accounting discipline stood out: Dan Norris grew revenue by 19% while wages and consumables surged by 40%, masking losses because no outsider was auditing the reality. I realized the chapter's core lesson isn't about selling out to investors—it is a warning against treating your business as an informal mental ledger rather than a documented, legible system.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Yes, because the author respects hard financial discipline over toxic optimism, which makes me trust that the next chapter will offer pragmatic reality rather than empty motivational slogans.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Ground the "Monday" action steps with a brief, micro-scale example (like a solo bookkeeper, consultant, or tradesperson) alongside the $16-million brewery, showing how someone without an accountant or local business neighbors can practically run "the stranger's read."

===== checks/readers/27-built-to-leave/pro-lagos.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
My first thought was, "Here we go again, another book about guys raising millions." I almost checked out when they started talking about $67 million valuations and corporate regulators because that is a completely different universe from selling clothes on Instagram in Lagos. But by the end, a genuine panic set in: if I get sick tomorrow, my business literally dies because it only exists in my head and my WhatsApp chat history. 

### 2. What landed
The reality check about pricing and absorbing costs hit hard: *"The business in six years just had one price increase."* In Nigeria, inflation and currency crashes are a daily reality; if I waited six years to raise prices, I would be bankrupt in three months. 

Also, the brutal truth of this line: *"the business will be worth exactly what a stranger can run and read. Not what you know about it."* Right now, my entire inventory, supplier list, and customer preferences are just me scrolling through my DMs and trusting my memory. If my phone gets stolen in traffic, my "asset" goes to zero. The distinction between a "practice" (buying myself a stressful job) and an "asset" (something that outlasts me) made me realize I've been lying to myself about what I'm building.

### 3. What didn't
The entire Black Hops case study felt alien. *"Black Hops at sixteen million dollars of revenue, with a board and a regulator and a crowd of shareholders, is not the reader's business..."* If the author knows it's not my business, why spend 70% of the chapter talking about it? I don't have a board, an external head of finance, or shareholders; I have a dispatch rider who stops picking up calls as soon as it starts raining. 

Furthermore, the Monday action item to *"Give the monthly page and the last two years' accounts to one person who has run a business... or the owner of the shop two doors down"* is a joke. This assumes a level of trust and formal business environment that doesn't exist in my market. You do not show your real numbers to strangers here unless you want your exact business model copied by next week or you want to be extorted.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I was too impatient and irritated by the corporate jargon (EBITDA, equity crowd-funding) to see the value. On the second read, I ignored the Australian brewery completely and focused on the mechanics of the "stranger's read." I realized that even at my tiny scale, the warning applies. I need to write down my supplier contacts, pricing margins, and standard responses on actual paper or a cloud doc, because right now my business has a catastrophic single point of failure: me.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
I would, but only because I'm desperate to see if they finally give practical steps on how to build these systems with zero budget, instead of just analyzing rich founders who messed up.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Scrap the massive brewery story and replace it with an example of a single-person, cash-strapped hustle that successfully documented its messy processes and transitioned into an asset, so readers at the bottom can actually see the bridge to the next level.

===== checks/readers/28-knowing-when-to-stop/codex-dev.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read

This is one of the rarer business-book chapters that treats shutting down as an operational decision instead of a character test. The core rule—attach a number and date to the final move—felt useful, but the chapter buried it under cross-references and a very long Netherlands-specific legal detour.

### 2. What landed — specific passages

“It is whether going on is a decision or the absence of one” landed hardest. I’ve watched teams keep projects alive because no one wanted to own the kill decision; I can easily imagine doing the same with my own money and employees involved.

“Every quarter spends something you will want later” also worked. Reserve, employee goodwill, and asset value are concrete things that decay while a founder tells himself he is being persistent.

The Binpress sequence was credible because it included actual numbers: revenue plateauing around $30K monthly after raising almost $1M, followed by multiple hires and a three-month pivot that changed nothing. “Most of the stuff we did had a positive effect” is especially believable: small improvements can disguise the absence of a viable trajectory.

Finally, “If [the move] has not moved [the figure] by [the date]” is actionable. That resembles how I’d define an engineering rollback threshold or terminate an experiment before sunk-cost reasoning takes over.

### 3. What didn’t

The Dutch winding-up box lost me almost immediately. “De turboliquidatie kun je niet gebruiken…” begins an enormous jurisdiction-specific section that excludes me as a US reader, despite explicitly telling me to skip it. Its length still interrupts the chapter’s momentum, and the raw `<em lang="nl">` fragments make it feel insufficiently edited.

The repeated references to “chapter sixteen’s sentence,” “chapter twenty-two’s three,” and “chapter twenty’s box” made the method feel unnecessarily dependent on remembering the author’s private vocabulary. By chapter 28 I might recognize it, but I should not have to reconstruct several earlier abstractions to understand a shutdown rule.

I also doubted “Binpress’s order is the one this book takes as general.” One founder’s retrospective can illustrate an order; it cannot establish that the order generalizes across bootstrapped companies, debt-heavy businesses, regulated firms, or US employment and bankruptcy law.

The “What would prove this chapter wrong” section sounds rigorous but largely immunizes the claim from evidence: the counter-case is declared “nearly unwritable,” then the rule survives because no published example appears.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two

On the second read, I noticed the author carefully excludes healthy, deliberately non-growing businesses; this is not generic “quit sooner” advice. I also appreciated the reserve calculation and pricing-the-parts exercise more. Conversely, the evidentiary gap became clearer: the practical rule is sensible, but Binpress does not prove that a quarter or two is generally the correct evaluation window.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?

Yes, if there were one, because the operational framing earns enough trust despite the overextended evidence.

### 6. One concrete thing I’d tell the author to change

Move the Dutch legal material to an appendix and replace it here with a one-page, jurisdiction-neutral shutdown checklist plus a clearly labeled instruction to obtain local legal and accounting advice.

===== checks/readers/28-knowing-when-to-stop/flash-cafe.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It gave me a knot in my stomach reading about closing down before I’ve even pulled my first espresso shot. But it’s refreshingly blunt—no cheerleading or motivational nonsense, just the cold reality of how businesses actually die of a slow bleed. Still, with all the talk of million-dollar seed rounds and Dutch tax laws, I wondered what on earth this had to do with opening a high-street café in Yorkshire.

### 2. What landed — specific passages and why they worked for someone like me
* **"It has not failed. That is what makes it hard. Customers still pay, the people still come in, the bank still clears the wages most months."** I’ve seen this happen to three different bosses over twenty-five years: you convince yourself everything is grand because the till rings on a Saturday, while quietly going under.
* **"stopping well costs money and it has to be there on the day."** That’s proper trade sense. If things go wrong, you need cash left in the tin to pay your veg supplier, settle with the staff, and hand back the keys with your head held high, rather than doing a midnight flit.
* **"the weekly conversation with yourself or your partner has had the same single item on it for longer than you would admit — what more can we do?"** That captures the sheer exhaustion of managing a struggling site better than any spreadsheet could.

### 3. What didn't — where I got bored, doubted the author, felt excluded or talked down to, or felt the advice didn't fit my situation
* **"we raised our seed round of almost $1M"** and **"a full pivot, to become 'the Shopify for digital products'"**. This is Monopoly money and tech jargon. If my footfall drops, I can’t "pivot" to an online software marketplace; I’m stuck with a five-year commercial lease, a bank loan, and an espresso machine.
* **"De turboliquidatie kun je niet gebruiken voor het beëindigen van een eenmanszaak..."** Dumping half a page of untranslated Dutch corporate law into the middle of the text completely derailed my concentration and felt totally irrelevant.
* **"Take chapter thirteen's cash... and divide it by the gap between a month's outgoings, counted as chapter sixteen counted them..."** Constant cross-referencing like an algebra textbook makes my eyes glaze over.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, I nearly tossed it aside as another Silicon Valley manual written for app developers who play with other people's millions. On the second read, I noticed the candid note at the back where the author openly admits software founders are just the only ones who publish their figures, and that the case study had a huge safety net most readers don't get. I appreciated that rare honesty, even if the core example still felt completely disconnected from daily trading.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
Since this is the final chapter, I’d actually go back to the beginning and read Chapter 4 to figure out my own day-one stopping rule before I sign my lease.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Shove the Dutch liquidation paperwork into an appendix, and replace the software startup example with an ordinary high-street business—like a bakery, pub, or dry cleaner—so those of us putting our redundancy money into physical premises can actually see ourselves in the advice.

===== checks/readers/28-knowing-when-to-stop/flash-refugee.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It felt sobering, disciplined, and remarkably free from the toxic "never give up" hustle mythology that ignores the reality of paying rent. Seeing specific Dutch administrative terms (*KVK*, *DigiD*, *Belastingdienst*) made my pulse spike, because navigating foreign bureaucracy under temporary protection is constant background stress. Yet the core realization—that drifting without a predefined exit is a quiet disaster—felt painfully practical.

### 2. What landed
*   **"Stopping well costs money and it has to be there on the day."** For someone with limited savings and family obligations, this is vital. I cannot afford the luxury of running an account to absolute zero; I need cash held back to wind down cleanly and feed my household.
*   **"Exhausted options are a date, not a feeling."** Coming from a finance and marketing background, I know how emotional attachment blinds people into repeating *"what more can we do?"* Tying the decision to a cold calendar date removes the personal shame.
*   **The practical Dutch box:** Noting that for an *eenmanszaak*, *"Het uitschrijven is gratis"* via DigiD, but *"Blijf je aangiftes doen zolang deze in Mijn Belastingdienst Zakelijk staan."* Knowing the administrative steps and tax duties upfront demystifies the legal risk of starting.

### 3. What didn't
*   **The tech startup scale:** Reading about Binpress where *"we raised our seed round of almost $1M"* and spent half of it trying out five specialized hires felt completely detached from my reality. A venture-backed software marketplace burning investor cash on enterprise salespeople does not reflect the stakes of a solo migrant starting a small consultancy with basic savings.
*   **Mechanical cross-referencing:** In the *Monday* section, phrasing like *"sixteen's throw-off or one of chapter twenty-two's three"* and *"counted as chapter sixteen counted them"* felt tedious and abstract, reducing an urgent, high-stakes human decision into textbook accounting jargon.

### 4. What changed between read one and read two
On the first read, the Dutch legal box felt overwhelming and the million-dollar case study irritated me. On the second read, I caught the author’s note in the *Reading* section explicitly acknowledging that Binpress was in a *"better-resourced position than most readers"* and that only the sequence of stopping applies, not the financial scale. I also saw that the Dutch box was actually reassuring: it clearly showed that an *eenmanszaak* avoids the heavy liabilities and creditor traps of a corporate *turboliquidatie*.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter?
This is the final chapter of the book, but this conclusion convinced me to go back and read Chapter 4 on setting the initial stopping rule and Chapter 12 on legal forms.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change
Include a small, self-funded solo business (an *eenmanszaak* or service provider) alongside the venture-backed tech company, showing how an owner without outside capital calculates their stopping date when their personal living reserve and business cash are tightly linked.

===== checks/readers/28-knowing-when-to-stop/pro-lagos.md =====
### 1. Gut reaction after first read
It felt like reading a manual for Silicon Valley tech bros while I’m out here trying to pay for diesel just to keep my phone charged and my ring light on. The scale of the money made me roll my eyes, but underneath all that grammar, the core warning about bleeding out slowly without a hard deadline actually struck a nerve.

### 2. What landed — specific passages (quote briefly) and why they worked for someone like me.
The absolute best part was: "Exhausted options are a date, not a feeling." Running an IG boutique in Lagos means running on vibes, prayers, and sheer stubbornness. I am always hoping the next bale clears customs faster, or the next promoted post goes viral. The realization that "Every answer costs another quarter, and every quarter spends something you will want later" felt like a slap in the face. My "reserve" isn't venture capital; it's my personal savings losing value to inflation every single day I stay stuck in the "what more can we do?" phase without a hard stop date.

### 3. What didn't — where I got bored, doubted the author, felt excluded or talked down to, or felt the advice didn't fit my situation. Quote the spot.
I completely checked out during the Binpress story. Quote: "We had spent a little over half the money we raised... we raised our seed round of almost $1M." Who is "we"? I started this with my own pocket money. They hired a "developer evangelist"; I am fighting with dispatch riders on WhatsApp. Then there’s the whole attempt at "selling the assets only (site and code-base)" and the massive gray box about "On Dutch ground" laws. I felt totally excluded. The author assumes formal structures, willing corporate buyers, and a legal safety net that simply do not exist in my reality. If my business fails, I just sell my leftover clothes at a heavy discount and delete the Instagram page. 

### 4. What changed between read one and read two.
On the first read, the startup jargon and that weird Dutch legal section annoyed me so much that I almost missed the point. On the second read, I forced myself to look past the examples, and I realized the "Monday" action list at the bottom is actually useful. Even though I don't have "a lease" or "code" to sell, the instruction to "Price the parts now, while they are worth something" made me think about my unsold inventory. Valuing what my dead stock would fetch *today* rather than waiting until I'm desperate is smart, practical advice.

### 5. Would I keep reading the next chapter? One sentence.
Since the text says this is chapter 28 of 28, there is no next chapter, but if there was, I'd skip it unless it dropped the tech-bro fantasies and talked about a real cash-based hustle.

### 6. One concrete thing I'd tell the author to change.
Ditch the million-dollar software startup case studies and use a normal, cash-constrained retail business as an example so everyday founders don't feel completely alienated by the second page.

