+# Reader-report digest: chapters 13–28
+
+## 13-profit-is-not-cash
+
+**Disagreements** — The Dutch material splits readers. The refugee treats “art. 6:119a lid 6 caps payment terms at 30 days” as evidence of protection; the café reader also accepts “Quoting the Burgerlijk Wetboek…is a way to be paid once” as lived commercial realism. Dev calls the statutory passage “dead weight,” Lagos says the statute describes a world where debtors do not simply block you, and the refugee finds the same diplomacy warning patronising.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — Refugee, café, Lagos, and dev all flag the Instacart material—“$358 million tax benefit” / “$373 million”—as overlarge, overlong, or unexplained relative to a small firm’s cash gap.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** replace corporate/statutory bulk with a documented small-business timeline.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** replace Instacart with a €10,000 local-business cash gap.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** shorten Instacart and add a deposits/stock/transfer example.
+- Café — **DUP:** shorten Instacart and foreground retail takings and VAT.
+
+## 13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice
+
+**Disagreements** — “This is the one chapter…whose case is an absence” reads as methodological restraint to dev and, on rereading, refugee; café hears stage-talk, while Lagos feels classified as leftover evidence. “No figure…is a claim about the world” raises dev’s trust in the caveat but makes Lagos ask why the figures should govern restocking.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — Café, Lagos, and dev reject “read against the same week last year”: a new business has no last year, inflation destroys comparability, and some demand is event-driven.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — label rules as derived or heuristic; remove unexplained durations.
+- Refugee — interview three immigrant cash-business operators instead of apologising for no case.
+- Lagos — use a phone-and-transfer worked week, without last-year comparisons.
+- Café — put Monday first and provide a week-one alternative to last-year data.
+
+## 14-what-a-customer-is-worth
+
+**Disagreements** — “Thirty dollars a month against four thousand up front” gives café a complete business shape, and Lagos follows the Cydoc arithmetic cleanly. Refugee experiences the “$4,000 to $6,000” EHR integration as exclusionary Silicon Valley scale; dev says the per-doctor/per-practice units make the asserted eleven-year payback unproven.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag the Cydoc case: it is a seven-year medical-software example with no ordinary cash-business bridge, and its displayed denominator is unclear.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — make doctor count and the eleven-year equation explicit.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** replace or balance Cydoc with an ordinary service business.
+- Lagos — add unstable-cost/currency treatment.
+- Café — **DUP:** add a no-subscription, unnamed-customer example.
+
+## 15-paying-yourself
+
+**Disagreements** — The Dutch rule “ten minste €58.000 in 2026” initially terrifies refugee, but on rereading proves the distinction between a BV and an eenmanszaak; café also extracts the underlying discipline. Lagos and dev experience the same extended tax material as foreign code that crowds out the sole-trader method.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag “Article 12a of the Wet op de loonbelasting 1964” and the untranslated quotations as disproportionate; café and dev also connect that space to the missing worked owner case.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** replace most statute with an end-to-end founder case.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** move the tax box to an appendix.
+- Lagos — give the sole trader equal space, including informal rates and inflation.
+- Café — **DUP:** cut quotations and show one owner’s role-by-role arithmetic.
+
+## 16-enough
+
+**Disagreements** — “Subtract what already covers it” is decisive evidence to café (husband’s wage/pension) and Lagos (family support). Dev objects to the nearby assumption that “almost every reader…has some” other income, because founders may be sole earners and partner income may not be dependable. “Time is the second ledger” is central to dev and Lagos but, for café, receives only a stub despite being the likely breaking point.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag Pinboard’s “$117K, $178K…” history as an unusually easy, low-cost solo-software case that cannot carry premises, payroll, dependants, or unstable currency.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** replace half of Pinboard with a worked developer side-SaaS.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** add a traditional business with overhead and dependants.
+- Lagos — explain how inflation changes the floor and review cadence.
+- Café — **DUP:** show the five-line calculation for staff, stock, and lease.
+
+## 17-you-are-the-bottleneck
+
+**Disagreements** — “Automation can exchange visible labour for concealed dependence” is direct evidence to dev, who recognises undocumented engineering systems. Café hears “the developer’s instinct” as reading over someone’s shoulder; Lagos reduces its automation to saved replies, and refugee feels excluded by PHP/MySQL assumptions.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag the two software cases—“core code…dated back to 2009–2010”—as overlong or non-probative; the chapter itself concedes neither owner tested absence.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — replace half the cases with a completed solo-software absence table.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** substitute a traditional shop, consultancy, or practice.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** add a non-Western owner whose accounts cannot be transferred.
+- Café — **DUP:** use premises/staff examples throughout the categories and Monday list.
+
+## 18-writing-it-down
+
+**Disagreements** — “The dividing line is whether the task is the same shape twice” gives refugee, café, and Lagos a workable procedure/judgment sort. Dev says it is too binary: incident response and support repeat yet still contain transferable heuristics, so the split may preserve dependency.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — Refugee, café, and Lagos flag “over 10 FTE,” “self-funded B2B SaaS,” and deploy-script language as an office/software frame remote from a first informal helper.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — show one complete handoff, failure, measurements, and revision.
+- Refugee — replace Gymdesk with a physical or professional-service business.
+- Lagos — make the test work in WhatsApp without an office or Drive.
+- Café — foreground worker-written procedures with a non-software example.
+
+## 19-the-first-hire
+
+**Disagreements** — “If a deliverable is late, you bought a task. If the day does not run, you have a job” gives refugee, café, and Lagos an immediate classification test. Dev calls it too neat: continuity, control, substitutability, and legal status do not collapse into one binary.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — Café, Lagos, and dev flag the Dutch box and its thresholds reappearing after “skip to Monday.” All four also find Gymdesk thin or badly scaled evidence for the hiring rule.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — replace the binary with a multi-factor table; localise the statutes.
+- Refugee — replace Gymdesk with a modest service business.
+- Lagos — adjust the low-month test for outages/devaluation and no accountant.
+- Café — cover a pre-opening first hire with no contractor or trading history.
+
+## 20-managing-people
+
+**Disagreements** — The Dutch requirement that an employee be “told in good time, given a real chance to improve” becomes decisive evidence to refugee because it overturns Groove’s consulting-trial model. Café, Lagos, and dev experience the untranslated box as exclusion; café notes its argument is nonetheless buried inside law she cannot read.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag Groove’s “2–4 week paid trial” as weakly generalisable or legally mismatched. All also flag the long Dutch section, though refugee wants its warning earlier rather than discarded.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** shorten the statute and add contrasting fast/slow-start cases.
+- Refugee — put the Dutch-law incompatibility before the Groove story.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** move the box out and address places without effective enforcement.
+- Café — **DUP:** add a non-Dutch legal warning before the box.
+
+## 21-meetings-that-arent-theater
+
+**Disagreements** — “A meeting held at the same time regardless” is evidence for refugee and Lagos that issues surface before urgency. Café says “same time every working day” excludes staggered shifts—“whose start?”—and dev says some incidents and blockers cannot be batched.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag the single remote-software case—nine people at screens, “two months and a feeling”—as thin evidence for physical, tiny, or shift-based businesses.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — add an interruption triage rule.
+- Refugee — use a physical shop or local service case.
+- Lagos — develop the two-person, group-chat version.
+- Café — handle staggered shifts and absent workers.
+
+## 22-measuring-the-right-three-things
+
+**Disagreements** — ConvertKit’s “net revenue up, MRR down, churn up and then down again” is useful evidence to dev that correct metrics can conflict. Refugee, café, and Lagos experience MRR, quick ratio, and million-dollar subscription months as a language barrier that precedes the admission most readers do not need it.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag the long ConvertKit inventory—“Quick Ratio = …,” Buffer, Hubstaff, ARPU—as SaaS research later discarded or insufficiently translated.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** replace half the case with a worked non-subscription example.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** use a bakery or accounting firm for the three measures.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** state buyer-language measures before ConvertKit.
+- Café — **DUP:** state cash-business measures before ConvertKit.
+
+## 23-slack
+
+**Disagreements** — “Put the fortnight in the calendar…and take it” is café’s real diagnostic and dev’s strongest exercise. Refugee calls deliberately interrupting income ridiculous; Lagos says disappearing for fourteen days means customers buy elsewhere and asks for two days first.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag WP Curve—“$23,535” MRR, fifteen staff, Philippine labour—as remote from a small physical or solo business and as evidence that never shows reserve surviving a shock.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — calculate reserve options against a named shock.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** replace WP Curve with a traditional small business.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** use a no-staff owner and a two-day drill.
+- Café — **DUP:** lead with a physical-business shock before WP Curve.
+
+## 24-what-you-signed
+
+**Disagreements** — “Move your terms from the invoice to the quote” is actionable evidence to dev, refugee, and Lagos. Café says it excludes counter-service hospitality, where nobody receives either. The Dutch copyright default supports refugee’s risk reading, while dev, café, and Lagos treat the untranslated statute as an access barrier.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag raw text such as “Het auteursrecht is het uitsluitend recht…” as disproportionate or inaccessible, including refugee, who values the legal substance but wants a parallel gloss.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** shorten the Dutch box; add a fully worked document dispute.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** add English paraphrases and lower-cost legal entry points.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** replace untranslated law with a universal small-business case.
+- Café — **DUP:** move statutes to an appendix; use counter-service examples.
+
+## 25-what-protects-you
+
+**Disagreements** — “A copy takes the customers who were never choosing you” is evidence to Lagos and café that relationships outlast copied products. Dev calls it much too confident because distribution, bundling, switching costs, and capital can take deliberate customers; refugee says constant outrunning assumes time and capital she lacks.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag Studio Neat’s “funding goal eighty-five minutes” and patent repetition as remote, incomplete evidence: it reports a decision, not enforcement cost or outcome.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** add a contrasting enforced or missing-IP case with outcome.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** add a modest European service/B2B client-list case.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** use a regular retail/service copying case.
+- Café — **DUP:** use a bakery, mechanic, or café case.
+
+## 26-when-the-ground-moves
+
+**Disagreements** — “The cut…comes on that date…not in week one on the fear” is stabilising evidence to café and refugee. Dev says discovery and reversible cuts can run concurrently; Lagos says doubled dispatch costs require a Monday-afternoon response, not a runway date.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag Gymdesk’s software/subscription frame and incomplete chronology as inadequate evidence for physical firms with rent, wages, stock, or transactional demand.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — replace the blanket delay with a runway/reversibility decision table.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** use a documented low-margin non-software shock.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** add a cash-based physical-goods translation.
+- Café — **DUP:** use a worked high-street shop or kitchen case.
+
+## 27-built-to-leave
+
+**Disagreements** — “The business in six years just had one price increase” is evidence to café, refugee, and Lagos that the owner absorbed rising costs. Dev says one increase proves no such thing: automation, purchasing leverage, mix, or deliberate margins could explain it.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag Black Hops’ “$67 million” valuation as overlarge and inconclusive. Dev, café, and refugee also flag shorthand such as “chapter twenty-two’s figures” as syllabus management rather than legible context.
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** shorten Black Hops and show a one-page handover.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** add a micro-scale stranger’s-read example.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** replace the brewery with a documented solo hustle.
+- Café — **DUP:** replace it with a high-street business and remove chapter-number callbacks.
+
+## 28-knowing-when-to-stop
+
+**Disagreements** — The Dutch box—“Het uitschrijven is gratis” and continuing tax filings—reassures refugee by making an eenmanszaak exit concrete. Dev, café, and Lagos experience “De turboliquidatie…” and the corporate winding-up detail as a long, untranslated exclusion.
+
+**Consensus complaints** — All four flag Binpress’s “seed round of almost $1M” as an over-resourced software case. Dev, café, and refugee also reject algebraic callbacks such as “chapter sixteen’s…chapter twenty-two’s three.”
+
+**Item 6 tally**
+
+- Dev — **DUP:** move Dutch law to an appendix; add a neutral shutdown checklist.
+- Refugee — **DUP:** add a self-funded solo stopping-date example.
+- Lagos — **DUP:** replace Binpress with cash-constrained retail.
+- Café — **DUP:** move Dutch law and use a high-street closure case.
+
+## Recurring across chapters
+
+- **Tech/SaaS or outsize cases exclude non-tech readers** — chapters 13, 14, 16–23, and 25–28. Refugee, café, and Lagos repeatedly have to translate Instacart, Cydoc, Pinboard, Gymdesk, ConvertKit, WP Curve, Studio Neat, Binpress, or large-company figures into local services, retail, hospitality, or phone-based trade. Dev often shares the scale/evidence objection, though software mechanisms themselves are legible to him.
